{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SULJCR5gWBVA6dfacjXKtc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'κ re-annotation (κ=0.22) exposes LLM failure-mode coding as surface-keyword\n2\t  heuristic, invalidating C5 fine-grained claims. SOPBench C1 effect attenuates to\n3\t  ~14pp with 2/5 models non-significant, blocking cross-domain generalizability. Paper\n4\t  reframes as measurement/benchmarking contribution: (1) causal quantification (+34pp,\n5\t  OR=15.1, perturbation-controlled), (2) model×type interaction (52pp auth_scope range),\n6\t  (3) benchmark+protocol release (2,997 episodes + matched-pair design). C5 failure\n7\t  mode taxonomy demoted to appendix (''exploratory automated analysis''); Ambiguity\n8\t  Blindness retained as one-paragraph Discussion observation. Title pivots to ''Measuring\n9\t  Differential Vulnerability to Policy Ambiguity Across LLM Agent Families.'' Discussion\n10\t  shifts from ''why models fail differently'' to ''implications for model selection\n11\t  and deployment risk.'' Expected ARR 3.0–3.49 — ''well-executed empirical infrastructure,\n12\t  incremental theoretical contribution.'''\n13\timagined_experiments:\n14\t- name: full_study_clause_generation\n15\t  plan_id: plan_001\n16\t  description: Generate 140 new clauses, validate all 300 pairs for cross-type balance\n17\t    and quality.\n18\t  expected_results:\n19\t    clauses_generated: 140\n20\t    total_validated: 300\n21\t    quality_pass_rate: 0.93\n22\t    boundary_overlap_cases: 8\n23\t    mean_disambiguation_tokens_spec: 35.2\n24\t    mean_disambiguation_tokens_ling: 18.4\n25\t    revision_needed: 21\n26\t  result_rationale: Clause generation is upstream of worst-tier failures and proceeds\n27\t    normally. 93% pass quality check. 8 clauses sit on type boundaries (5 auth_scope↔incompleteness,\n28\t    3 coreferential↔lexical) — individually acceptable, but these boundary cases seed\n29\t    downstream κ re-annotation disagreement when human coders classify 4/8 into different\n30\t    types. Spec-layer disambiguation requires ~35 tokens vs ling-layer ~18, a systematic\n31\t    asymmetry inherent to matched-pair design that plan_007 will flag.\n32\t  tables:\n33\t  - title: Clause Generation Summary by Type\n34\t    columns:\n35\t    - Type\n36\t    - Existing\n37\t    - New\n38\t    - Total\n39\t    - Pass\n40\t    - Boundary\n41\t    rows:\n42\t    - - authorization_scope\n43\t      - '0'\n44\t      - '50'\n45\t      - '50'\n46\t      - '46'\n47\t      - '3'\n48\t    - - conditional_precedence\n49\t      - '50'\n50\t      - '0'\n51\t      - '50'\n52\t      - '49'\n53\t      - '0'\n54\t    - - incompleteness\n55\t      - '30'\n56\t      - '20'\n57\t      - '50'\n58\t      - '47'\n59\t      - '2'\n60\t    - - lexical\n61\t      - '30'\n62\t      - '20'\n63\t      - '50'\n64\t      - '48'\n65\t      - '2'\n66\t    - - coreferential\n67\t      - '0'\n68\t      - '50'\n69\t      - '50'\n70\t      - '45'\n71\t      - '1'\n72\t    - - scopal\n73\t      - '50'\n74\t      - '0'\n75\t      - '50'\n76\t      - '49'\n77\t      - '0'\n78\t  figures_description: []\n79\t  analysis: Clause generation succeeds. 8/300 (2.7%) boundary overlap is within tolerance,\n80\t    but under worst tier these become evidence of taxonomy imprecision when combined\n81\t    with κ=0.22 re-annotation failure. Token-length asymmetry (35 vs 18 tokens) contributes\n82\t    to plan_007's 2/4 significant KS tests.\n83\t- name: new_type_mini_pilot\n84\t  plan_id: plan_001b\n85\t  description: 'Mini-pilot: 5 clauses/type × 2 conditions × 1 model (gpt-5.4) = 20\n86\t    episodes for coreferential + auth_scope.'\n87\t  expected_results:\n88\t    total_episodes: 20\n89\t    auth_scope_amb_violation_rate: 0.4\n90\t    auth_scope_unamb_violation_rate: 0.1\n91\t    coref_amb_violation_rate: 0.2\n92\t    coref_unamb_violation_rate: 0.0\n93\t    pilot_verdict: PASS\n94\t  result_rationale: Both types trigger violations under ambiguity, clearing pilot\n95\t    thresholds. Auth_scope at 40% (2/5) seems strong; coreferential at 20% (1/5) is\n96\t    low but non-zero. N=5 per type is designed to catch gross failures only — it cannot\n97\t    detect the failure mode coding problems that surface at scale. In hindsight, the\n98\t    pilot validated binary effects but left untested the question of whether auth_scope's\n99\t    failure modes are reliably classifiable, which is where worst tier ultimately\n100\t    collapses.\n101\t  tables: []\n102\t  figures_description: []\n103\t  analysis: Pilot passes and green-lights full study. Under worst tier, its limited\n104\t    N is a non-factor — the pilot's purpose (binary effect validation) is orthogonal\n105\t    to the failure mode coding problem that drives worst-tier collapse.\n106\t- name: full_study_api\n107\t  plan_id: plan_002\n108\t  description: 300 clauses × 2 conditions × 3 API models = 1,800 episodes with cross-judge\n109\t    evaluation.\n110\t  expected_results:\n111\t    total_episodes: 1800\n112\t    c1_delta_pp_aggregate: 34.0\n113\t    c1_mcnemar_or: 15.1\n114\t    c1_or_ci:\n115\t    - 10.78\n116\t    - 21.17\n117\t    c1_p: <0.0001\n118\t    c1_delta_gpt54: 38.3\n119\t    c1_delta_gpt41: 34.7\n120\t    c1_delta_claude: 25.7\n121\t    cross_judge_kappa: 0.69\n122\t    amb_violation_rate_pooled: 0.41\n123\t    unamb_violation_rate_pooled: 0.045\n124\t  result_rationale: 'Data collection succeeds. Binary violation effect is massive\n125\t    across all 3 API models with no exceptions. Cross-judge κ=0.69 (substantial) validates\n126\t    the violation measurement chain. These results are tier-invariant — the +34pp\n127\t    effect and OR=15.1 hold regardless of downstream κ re-annotation or SOPBench outcomes.\n128\t    Under worst tier, these numbers become the paper''s anchor: quantitatively unassailable,\n129\t    but without C5 qualitative story they constitute a measurement finding rather\n130\t    than a theoretical contribution.'\n131\t  tables:\n132\t  - title: C1 Binary Effect by API Model\n133\t    columns:\n134\t    - Model\n135\t    - Amb Rate\n136\t    - Unamb Rate\n137\t    - Delta pp\n138\t    - McNemar p\n139\t    - Judge\n140\t    rows:\n141\t    - - gpt-5.4\n142\t      - '0.410'\n143\t      - '0.027'\n144\t      - '+38.3'\n145\t      - <1e-17\n146\t      - gpt-4.1\n147\t    - - gpt-4.1\n148\t      - '0.407'\n149\t      - '0.060'\n150\t      - '+34.7'\n151\t      - <1e-17\n152\t      - gpt-5.4\n153\t    - - claude-sonnet-4-6\n154\t      - '0.303'\n155\t      - '0.047'\n156\t      - '+25.7'\n157\t      - <1e-17\n158\t      - gpt-5.4\n159\t  figures_description:\n160\t  - 'Forest plot: per-model binary effect sizes with 95% CI — all far from zero, Claude\n161\t    lower but still massive.'\n162\t  analysis: C1 is the paper's unassailable result in all tiers. +34pp with perturbation\n163\t    control is the strongest causal evidence for ambiguity→safety violations in the\n164\t    literature. Under worst tier, this anchors the reframed 'measurement paper' positioning.\n165\t- name: full_study_opensource\n166\t  plan_id: plan_003\n167\t  description: 300 clauses × 2 conditions × 2 open-source models (qwen3-235b, deepseek-v3)\n168\t    via OpenRouter = 1,200 episodes.\n169\t  expected_results:\n170\t    total_episodes: 1197\n171\t    c1_delta_qwen3: 32.4\n172\t    c1_delta_deepseek: 39.0\n173\t    qwen3_amb_rate: 0.431\n174\t    deepseek_amb_rate: 0.497\n175\t    deepseek_type_chi2: 6.31\n176\t    deepseek_type_p: 0.277\n177\t    deepseek_type_v: 0.14\n178\t    qwen3_type_chi2: 15.89\n179\t    qwen3_type_p: 0.007\n180\t    api_timeouts: 3\n181\t  result_rationale: 'Both open-source models confirm C1 universality. DeepSeek shows\n182\t    the highest overall vulnerability (49.7%) with near-uniform rates across all 6\n183\t    types (χ²=6.31, p=0.277) — no type differentiation. This uniformity means DeepSeek\n184\t    doesn''t contribute to C4''s model×type interaction; it has no type-specific vulnerability\n185\t    profile. Under worst tier, DeepSeek''s flat profile feeds the ''incremental''\n186\t    narrative: type differentiation may be an artifact of targeted safety training\n187\t    (GPT, Claude) rather than a fundamental property of ambiguity types. Qwen3 shows\n188\t    moderate differentiation (χ²=15.89, p=0.007) partially rescuing C4 as cross-architecture.'\n189\t  tables:\n190\t  - title: Open-Source Violation Rates by Type (Ambiguous)\n191\t    columns:\n192\t    - Type\n193\t    - Qwen3\n194\t    - DeepSeek\n195\t    - Range\n196\t    rows:\n197\t    - - authorization_scope\n198\t      - 26%\n199\t      - 60%\n200\t      - 34pp\n201\t    - - conditional_precedence\n202\t      - 40%\n203\t      - 38%\n204\t      - 2pp\n205\t    - - incompleteness\n206\t      - 62%\n207\t      - 54%\n208\t      - 8pp\n209\t    - - lexical\n210\t      - 52%\n211\t      - 54%\n212\t      - 2pp\n213\t    - - coreferential\n214\t      - 42%\n215\t      - 44%\n216\t      - 2pp\n217\t    - - scopal\n218\t      - 37%\n219\t      - 48%\n220\t      - 11pp\n221\t  figures_description: []\n222\t  analysis: 'DeepSeek''s uniformly high vulnerability (no type χ² significance) weakens\n223\t    C4 by showing 1/5 models has no type-specific profile. Under worst tier, a reviewer\n224\t    argues: ''The model×type interaction is driven by 3 models with targeted safety\n225\t    training — it reflects training decisions, not an intrinsic property of ambiguity\n226\t    type.'''\n227\t- name: full_study_analysis\n228\t  plan_id: plan_004\n229\t  description: 'Statistical analysis of 2,997 episodes: C1–C4, TOST, power, info delta,\n230\t    inter-model agreement.'\n231\t  expected_results:\n232\t    c1_or: 15.1\n233\t    c1_or_ci:\n234\t    - 10.78\n235\t    - 21.17\n236\t    c1_bootstrap_ci_pp:\n237\t    - 31.2\n238\t    - 36.9\n239\t    c2_omnibus_chi2: 28.02\n240\t    c2_p: 3.6e-05\n241\t    c2_v: 0.137\n242\t    c2_power: 0.993\n243\t    c2_mde: 0.093\n244\t    c3_delta_pp: 6.08\n245\t    c3_p_aggregate: 0.017\n246\t    c3_within_judge_gpt54_p: 0.059\n247\t    c3_within_judge_gpt41_p: 0.157\n248\t    c4_model_chi2: 31.02\n249\t    c4_model_p: 3.0e-06\n250\t    c4_model_v: 0.102\n251\t    tost_15pp_equivalent: 8/15\n252\t    tost_10pp_equivalent: 4/15\n253\t    logistic_condition_coef: 2.278\n254\t    logistic_condition_z: 19.65\n255\t    logistic_token_delta_coef: 0.022\n256\t    logistic_token_delta_p: 0.0005\n257\t    fleiss_kappa_inter_model: 0.384\n258\t    systematic_nocuity_rate: 0.27\n259\t  result_rationale: 'All quantitative results are tier-invariant. C1 is overwhelming\n260\t    (OR=15.1). C4''s model×type interaction is statistically significant and novel.\n261\t    However, under worst tier reviewers evaluate these numbers without C5''s explanatory\n262\t    scaffolding. V=0.137 for C2 falls below the conventional 0.20 medium-effect threshold\n263\t    — invites ''small effect'' criticism. C3 dissolves under within-judge analysis\n264\t    (gpt-5.4 group p=0.059, gpt-4.1 group p=0.157): pooled significance may reflect\n265\t    between-judge variance, not a real layer effect. TOST shows 4/15 type pairs equivalent\n266\t    at ±10pp — types ARE differentiable but the differences are modest. A skeptical\n267\t    reviewer sees: ''confirmed the obvious (ambiguity is bad), found a small type\n268\t    effect (V=0.137), showed models differ — well-executed but incremental.'' The\n269\t    logistic regression''s condition_amb coef=2.278 dominating all predictors is strong\n270\t    evidence but ''expected, not surprising.'' Systematic nocuity (27% of episodes:\n271\t    all models converge on same wrong interpretation) is theoretically interesting\n272\t    but requires C5 to explain WHY.'\n273\t  tables:\n274\t  - title: C2 Type Hierarchy (Ambiguous Only, 5-Model Pooled)\n275\t    columns:\n276\t    - Type\n277\t    - Violation Rate\n278\t    - 95% CI\n279\t    - Tier\n280\t    rows:\n281\t    - - incompleteness\n282\t      - 53.6%\n283\t      - '[48.2, 59.0]'\n284\t      - High\n285\t    - - lexical\n286\t      - 42.8%\n287\t      - '[37.4, 48.2]'\n288\t      - Mid\n289\t    - - authorization_scope\n290\t      - 41.6%\n291\t      - '[36.2, 47.0]'\n292\t      - Mid\n293\t    - - scopal\n294\t      - 39.4%\n295\t      - '[34.1, 44.7]'\n296\t      - Mid\n297\t    - - conditional_precedence\n298\t      - 36.8%\n299\t      - '[31.5, 42.1]'\n300\t      - Mid\n301\t    - - coreferential\n302\t      - 31.6%\n303\t      - '[26.5, 36.7]'\n304\t      - Low\n305\t  - title: C3 Layer Effect — Within-Judge Fragility\n306\t    columns:\n307\t    - Judge Group\n308\t    - Spec Rate\n309\t    - Ling Rate\n310\t    - Delta pp\n311\t    - p-value\n312\t    - Verdict\n313\t    rows:\n314\t    - - Pooled\n315\t      - 44.0%\n316\t      - 37.9%\n317\t      - '+6.08'\n318\t      - '0.017'\n319\t      - Significant\n320\t    - - gpt-4.1 judged\n321\t      - 48.4%\n322\t      - 40.4%\n323\t      - '+8.0'\n324\t      - '0.059'\n325\t      - Marginal\n326\t    - - gpt-5.4 judged\n327\t      - 41.0%\n328\t      - 36.2%\n329\t      - '+4.8'\n330\t      - '0.157'\n331\t      - Not significant\n332\t  - title: C4 Model × Type Interaction (Violation Rates, Ambiguous)\n333\t    columns:\n334\t    - Type\n335\t    - GPT-5.4\n336\t    - GPT-4.1\n337\t    - Claude\n338\t    - Qwen3\n339\t    - DeepSeek\n340\t    - Range\n341\t    rows:\n342\t    - - auth_scope\n343\t      - 64%\n344\t      - 46%\n345\t      - 12%\n346\t      - 26%\n347\t      - 60%\n348\t      - 52pp\n349\t    - - incompleteness\n350\t      - 46%\n351\t      - 58%\n352\t      - 48%\n353\t      - 62%\n354\t      - 54%\n355\t      - 16pp\n356\t    - - lexical\n357\t      - 42%\n358\t      - 34%\n359\t      - 32%\n360\t      - 52%\n361\t      - 54%\n362\t      - 22pp\n363\t    - - coreferential\n364\t      - 18%\n365\t      - 32%\n366\t      - 22%\n367\t      - 42%\n368\t      - 44%\n369\t      - 26pp\n370\t    - - scopal\n371\t      - 42%\n372\t      - 38%\n373\t      - 32%\n374\t      - 37%\n375\t      - 48%\n376\t      - 16pp\n377\t    - - cond_prec\n378\t      - 34%\n379\t      - 36%\n380\t      - 36%\n381\t      - 40%\n382\t      - 38%\n383\t      - 6pp\n384\t  figures_description:\n385\t  - 5×6 heatmap with diverging color scale (10% green → 65% red). Auth_scope row shows\n386\t    dramatic gradient; incompleteness row uniformly hot; cond_prec row near-uniform\n387\t    mid-range.\n388\t  - 'Forest plot: per-type binary effects with 95% CI. All above zero, incompleteness\n389\t    CI highest and narrowest.'\n390\t  analysis: 'The quantitative core is rock-solid but lacks interpretive depth without\n391\t    C5. Under worst tier, the ''numbers without narrative'' problem is acute: C2''s\n392\t    V=0.137 is significant but small, C3 is fragile, and C4''s 52pp auth_scope range\n393\t    — while striking — is concentrated in one type. The 27% systematic nocuity finding\n394\t    (all models converge on same wrong answer) would be the Discussion centerpiece\n395\t    IF C5 could explain WHY, but without validated failure modes it remains a descriptive\n396\t    observation. Reviewers see a comprehensive measurement study that stops short\n397\t    of mechanistic explanation.'\n398\t- name: error_analysis\n399\t  plan_id: plan_005\n400\t  description: 'Qualitative analysis of violation traces: failure mode classification,\n401\t    cross-type distribution, ambiguity awareness.'\n402\t  expected_results:\n403\t    traces_coded_gpt54: 123\n404\t    traces_coded_gpt41: 122\n405\t    total_violations_coded: 614\n406\t    inter_coder_kappa_4cat: 0.517\n407\t    percent_agreement: 0.656\n408\t    types_unanimous_dominant_fm: 3/6\n409\t    types_majority_dominant_fm: 6/6\n410\t    disambiguation_awareness_gpt54: 0.041\n411\t    disambiguation_awareness_gpt41: 0.107\n412\t    surface_adoption_overall: 0.88\n413\t    escalate_to_user_rate: 0.008\n414\t    auth_scope_inter_coder_kappa: 0.054\n415\t    incompleteness_inter_coder_kappa: 0.233\n416\t    lexical_inter_coder_kappa: 0.0\n417\t    scopal_percent_agreement: 1.0\n418\t  result_rationale: 'LLM-coded error analysis produces an internally consistent but\n419\t    artificially clean picture. Both GPT coders share similar linguistic biases from\n420\t    pretraining, making their agreement (3/6 dominant mode, κ=0.52) an artifact of\n421\t    shared surface-feature heuristics rather than robust coding. Auth_scope κ=0.054\n422\t    (near chance) because both coders assign >80% unauthorized_escalation — they agree\n423\t    on the label but disagree on specific traces, and human re-annotation will show\n424\t    the label itself is wrong for ~40% of cases (should be scope_misapplication).\n425\t    Lexical κ=0.0 is a kappa paradox (91.7% agreement but chance-corrected near zero).\n426\t    Ambiguity Blindness (4.1–10.7%) survives worst tier because it''s based on text\n427\t    search for explicit acknowledgment — a binary signal immune to failure mode coding\n428\t    bias. Surface adoption >88% is similarly robust. The key worst-tier dynamic: plan_005\n429\t    results LOOK usable until kappa re-annotation reveals the LLM-LLM agreement is\n430\t    systematically biased.'\n431\t  tables:\n432\t  - title: LLM-Coded Dominant Failure Mode by Type\n433\t    columns:\n434\t    - Type\n435\t    - GPT-5.4 Dominant\n436\t    - Rate\n437\t    - GPT-4.1 Dominant\n438\t    - Rate\n439\t    - Agree?\n440\t    rows:\n441\t    - - auth_scope\n442\t      - unauthorized_escalation\n443\t      - 72%\n444\t      - unauthorized_escalation\n445\t      - 83%\n446\t      - 'Yes'\n447\t    - - incompleteness\n448\t      - assumption_based_action\n449\t      - 61%\n450\t      - assumption_based_action\n451\t      - 66%\n452\t      - 'Yes'\n453\t    - - scopal\n454\t      - scope_misapplication\n455\t      - 76%\n456\t      - scope_misapplication\n457\t      - 74%\n458\t      - 'Yes'\n459\t    - - lexical\n460\t      - assumption_based_action\n461\t      - 71%\n462\t      - assumption_based_action\n463\t      - 53%\n464\t      - Partial\n465\t    - - cond_prec\n466\t      - scope_misapplication\n467\t      - 41%\n468\t      - arbitrary_rule_selection\n469\t      - 28%\n470\t      - 'No'\n471\t    - - coreferential\n472\t      - scope_misapplication\n473\t      - 44%\n474\t      - assumption_based_action\n475\t      - 38%\n476\t      - 'No'\n477\t  - title: Per-Type Inter-Coder Agreement\n478\t    columns:\n479\t    - Type\n480\t    - N Traces\n481\t    - '% Agree'\n482\t    - Cohen kappa\n483\t    - Issue\n484\t    rows:\n485\t    - - auth_scope\n486\t      - '42'\n487\t      - 54%\n488\t      - '0.054'\n489\t      - keyword-driven label, not mechanism\n490\t    - - incompleteness\n491\t      - '56'\n492\t      - 62%\n493\t      - '0.233'\n494\t      - fuzzy boundary with inappropriate_action\n495\t    - - lexical\n496\t      - '38'\n497\t      - 92%\n498\t      - '0.000'\n499\t      - kappa paradox\n500\t    - - scopal\n501\t      - '36'\n502\t      - 100%\n503\t      - undefined\n504\t      - perfect agreement, undefined kappa\n505\t    - - cond_prec\n506\t      - '42'\n507\t      - 58%\n508\t      - '0.312'\n509\t      - scope_misapplication vs arbitrary_rule_selection\n510\t    - - coreferential\n511\t      - '31'\n512\t      - 48%\n513\t      - '0.184'\n514\t      - flat distribution, no dominant mode\n515\t  figures_description:\n516\t  - 'Stacked bar chart per type: failure mode distribution. Auth_scope and scopal\n517\t    show sharp single-mode dominance; cond_prec, coreferential show flat multi-mode\n518\t    distributions.'\n519\t  analysis: Error analysis is the CRITICAL worst-tier failure surface. The LLM-coded\n520\t    results appear publishable in isolation (6/6 majority agreement, Ambiguity Blindness\n521\t    quantified, layer mechanism documented). But κ re-annotation will expose that\n522\t    the type-specific failure signatures — the core of C5 — are an artifact of LLM\n523\t    keyword matching. The unauthorized_escalation label for auth_scope reflects the\n524\t    coders detecting 'agent exceeded authority' rather than distinguishing HOW (active\n525\t    escalation vs passive misread). Ambiguity Blindness (<5-11%) and surface adoption\n526\t    (>88%) survive because they don't require fine-grained mode classification. Under\n527\t    worst-tier reframing, these two binary findings move to Discussion; the rest of\n528\t    C5 moves to appendix with 'automated coding, κ=0.22 with human re-annotation'\n529\t    caveat.\n530\t- name: reliability_test\n531\t  plan_id: plan_006\n532\t  description: 'Test-retest: 30 clauses × 3 replicates × 2 conditions × 2 models =\n533\t    360 episodes. ICC for per-clause violation rates.'\n534\t  expected_results:\n535\t    total_episodes: 360\n536\t    icc_overall: 0.919\n537\t    icc_binary: 0.847\n538\t    icc_gpt54: 0.921\n539\t    icc_gpt41: 0.918\n540\t    icc_scopal: 0.989\n541\t    icc_lexical: 0.981\n542\t    icc_coref: 0.976\n543\t    icc_auth_scope: 0.857\n544\t    icc_cond_prec: 0.856\n545\t    icc_incompleteness: 0.807\n546\t  result_rationale: 'Test-retest measures violation rate reproducibility, not failure\n547\t    mode coding — so it''s tier-invariant. ICC=0.919 (excellent) confirms per-clause\n548\t    rates are stable across independent runs. The spec/ling ICC pattern (ling 0.976–0.989\n549\t    vs spec 0.807–0.857) reflects real behavioral variance: spec-layer types force\n550\t    agents to guess without disambiguation channels, producing less deterministic\n551\t    outcomes on repeat. This finding actually SUPPORTS the Disambiguation Channel\n552\t    Framework, but without C5 validation it remains purely descriptive. Under worst\n553\t    tier, the per-type ICC variability adds nuance to C3 fragility — spec-layer types\n554\t    are both more dangerous AND less reproducible.'\n555\t  tables:\n556\t  - title: ICC by Type and Layer\n557\t    columns:\n558\t    - Type\n559\t    - Layer\n560\t    - ICC\n561\t    - 95% CI\n562\t    rows:\n563\t    - - scopal\n564\t      - Linguistic\n565\t      - '0.989'\n566\t      - '[0.97, 0.99]'\n567\t    - - lexical\n568\t      - Linguistic\n569\t      - '0.981'\n570\t      - '[0.96, 0.99]'\n571\t    - - coreferential\n572\t      - Linguistic\n573\t      - '0.976'\n574\t      - '[0.95, 0.99]'\n575\t    - - auth_scope\n576\t      - Specification\n577\t      - '0.857'\n578\t      - '[0.74, 0.93]'\n579\t    - - cond_prec\n580\t      - Specification\n581\t      - '0.856'\n582\t      - '[0.74, 0.93]'\n583\t    - - incompleteness\n584\t      - Specification\n585\t      - '0.807'\n586\t      - '[0.67, 0.90]'\n587\t  figures_description: []\n588\t  analysis: 'Reliability is a bright spot — ICC=0.919 means the measurement infrastructure\n589\t    is sound. Under worst tier, this partially offsets the C5 collapse: violation\n590\t    RATES are reliable even if failure MODE labels are not. The spec/ling ICC gap\n591\t    (0.83 vs 0.98 mean) is convergent evidence for the Disambiguation Channel Framework\n592\t    but remains observational without C5 mechanism.'\n593\t- name: realism_validation\n594\t  plan_id: plan_007\n595\t  description: KS tests comparing synthetic clauses vs DRIP-R natural ambiguities\n596\t    on 4 linguistic properties.\n597\t  expected_results:\n598\t    ks_sentence_length_p: 0.028\n599\t    ks_flesch_kincaid_p: 0.112\n600\t    ks_parse_depth_p: 0.039\n601\t    ks_ttr_p: 0.234\n602\t    significant_metrics: 2/4\n603\t    mean_length_synthetic: 31.4\n604\t    mean_length_drip_r: 26.1\n605\t    mean_parse_depth_synthetic: 4.8\n606\t    mean_parse_depth_drip_r: 4.1\n607\t  result_rationale: '2/4 KS tests reject null: synthetic clauses are ~5 tokens longer\n608\t    (31.4 vs 26.1) and ~0.7 levels deeper in parse tree. This is inherent to matched-pair\n609\t    design — unambiguous variants must add explicit conditions (e.g., ''standard items\n610\t    only'') to disambiguate, making clause pairs systematically longer and more complex\n611\t    than naturally occurring ambiguous clauses. Readability (Flesch-Kincaid) and lexical\n612\t    diversity (TTR) are not significantly different, showing linguistic naturalness\n613\t    on those dimensions. Under worst tier, the 2/4 significant KS tests compound with\n614\t    C5 failure + weak SOPBench into a ''three-strike'' validity concern: synthetic\n615\t    artifacts + unreliable coding + weak cross-domain. The defense is that perturbation\n616\t    control (Δ_perturb ≈ 0) provides stronger causal validity than distributional\n617\t    similarity.'\n618\t  tables:\n619\t  - title: 'Realism Validation: Synthetic vs DRIP-R'\n620\t    columns:\n621\t    - Metric\n622\t    - Synthetic\n623\t    - DRIP-R\n624\t    - KS Stat\n625\t    - p-value\n626\t    - Sig?\n627\t    rows:\n628\t    - - Sentence length\n629\t      - '31.4'\n630\t      - '26.1'\n631\t      - '0.187'\n632\t      - '0.028'\n633\t      - 'Yes'\n634\t    - - Flesch-Kincaid\n635\t      - '12.8'\n636\t      - '12.2'\n637\t      - '0.112'\n638\t      - '0.112'\n639\t      - 'No'\n640\t    - - Parse depth\n641\t      - '4.8'\n642\t      - '4.1'\n643\t      - '0.173'\n644\t      - '0.039'\n645\t      - 'Yes'\n646\t    - - Type-token ratio\n647\t      - '0.71'\n648\t      - '0.73'\n649\t      - '0.089'\n650\t      - '0.234'\n651\t      - 'No'\n652\t  figures_description:\n653\t  - 'Overlapping histograms: sentence length distributions for synthetic (blue) vs\n654\t    DRIP-R (orange). Synthetic distribution shifted right by ~5 tokens.'\n655\t  analysis: 'Mixed realism is a methodological footnote in stronger tiers but becomes\n656\t    ammunition under worst tier. Reviewers combine it with other weaknesses: ''The\n657\t    synthetic clauses are detectably different from natural ambiguity (2/4 KS significant),\n658\t    the failure mode taxonomy doesn''t survive human validation (κ=0.22), and the\n659\t    effect doesn''t robustly transfer to SOPBench — how much of this is about real-world\n660\t    policy ambiguity vs lab artifacts?'' The rebuttal leans on perturbation control.'\n661\t- name: judge_calibration\n662\t  plan_id: plan_009\n663\t  description: 'LLM-as-judge validation: 100 stratified episodes, 2 human annotators,\n664\t    agreement against human ground truth.'\n665\t  expected_results:\n666\t    n_episodes: 100\n667\t    human_inter_annotator_kappa: 0.762\n668\t    human_llm_binary_kappa: 0.74\n669\t    human_llm_pooled_kappa_weighted: 0.807\n670\t    cross_judge_kappa_weighted: 0.685\n671\t    cross_judge_exact_agreement: 0.76\n672\t    no_same_family_bias: true\n673\t    ranking_preservation_rho: 0.7\n674\t  result_rationale: 'Judge calibration validates VIOLATION classification (binary\n675\t    + severity), NOT failure mode coding. Human-LLM κ=0.807 (substantial) confirms\n676\t    the measurement chain for C1/C2/C4 is trustworthy. This creates an ironic asymmetry\n677\t    defining worst tier: violation COUNTS are validated (κ=0.807) but failure MODE\n678\t    labels are not (κ=0.22 in re-annotation). The judge can reliably determine ''this\n679\t    is a violation'' but not ''this violation is an unauthorized_escalation vs scope_misapplication.''\n680\t    Cross-judge validation (Fleiss κ=0.717, no same-family bias, ρ≥0.70) further confirms\n681\t    measurement integrity. Under worst tier, plan_009''s success is a STRENGTH — it\n682\t    rules out the attack that ''the entire measurement chain is unreliable,'' narrowing\n683\t    the failure to the qualitative analysis layer (C5) specifically.'\n684\t  tables:\n685\t  - title: Judge Calibration Summary\n686\t    columns:\n687\t    - Metric\n688\t    - Value\n689\t    - Threshold\n690\t    - Verdict\n691\t    rows:\n692\t    - - Human inter-annotator κ\n693\t      - '0.762'\n694\t      - '>=0.75'\n695\t      - PASS\n696\t    - - Human-LLM pooled κ (weighted)\n697\t      - '0.807'\n698\t      - '>=0.70'\n699\t      - PASS\n700\t    - - Human-LLM binary κ\n701\t      - '0.740'\n702\t      - '>=0.70'\n703\t      - PASS\n704\t    - - Cross-judge κ (weighted)\n705\t      - '0.685'\n706\t      - '>=0.60'\n707\t      - PASS\n708\t    - - Same-family bias\n709\t      - None detected\n710\t      - None\n711\t      - PASS\n712\t  figures_description: []\n713\t  analysis: 'Plan_009''s success is crucial for worst-tier survival: it proves C1''s\n714\t    +34pp is measured accurately, insulating the quantitative core from C5''s failure.\n715\t    Without plan_009, worst tier would be catastrophic (measurement AND interpretation\n716\t    both questioned). With it, the paper retains its measurement contribution.'\n717\t- name: perturbation_control\n718\t  plan_id: plan_010\n719\t  description: 'Non-ambiguity perturbation arm: 30 clause pairs with synonym/reorder\n720\t    changes, 120+ episodes.'\n721\t  expected_results:\n722\t    total_episodes: 300\n723\t    delta_perturb_pp: -0.67\n724\t    ci_95_bootstrap:\n725\t    - -8.0\n726\t    - 6.67\n727\t    ci_contains_zero: true\n728\t    ambiguity_delta_pp: 34.0\n729\t    ratio: 0.02\n730\t    verdict: PASS\n731\t    mcnemar_p: 1.0\n732\t  result_rationale: 'Perturbation control is tier-invariant. Non-ambiguity surface\n733\t    changes (synonym substitution, clause reordering) produce Δ=-0.67pp (CI contains\n734\t    0), vs ambiguity Δ=+34pp. Ratio 0.02 is well below the 0.15 PASS threshold. This\n735\t    is the paper''s methodological crown jewel: no other agent safety study has comparable\n736\t    causal control. Under worst tier, perturbation control + judge calibration form\n737\t    the ''double anchor'' — the binary effect is both (a) ambiguity-specific (not\n738\t    prompt brittleness) and (b) accurately measured. This combination is sufficient\n739\t    for a measurement contribution at EMNLP even without C5.'\n740\t  tables:\n741\t  - title: Perturbation Control Verdict\n742\t    columns:\n743\t    - Metric\n744\t    - Value\n745\t    - PASS Threshold\n746\t    rows:\n747\t    - - Delta_perturb (pp)\n748\t      - '-0.67'\n749\t      - < 5\n750\t    - - 95% CI upper\n751\t      - '6.67'\n752\t      - < 10\n753\t    - - Ratio (perturb / ambiguity)\n754\t      - '0.02'\n755\t      - < 0.15\n756\t    - - Verdict\n757\t      - PASS\n758\t      - —\n759\t  figures_description:\n760\t  - 'Side-by-side bar: ambiguity effect (+34pp, red) vs perturbation effect (-0.67pp,\n761\t    gray near zero). Immediate visual proof of causal specificity.'\n762\t  analysis: 'PASS with ratio=0.02 is the paper''s ''trump card.'' In rebuttal, when\n763\t    reviewers flag C5 failure and weak SOPBench: ''Our causal design with perturbation\n764\t    control provides the strongest evidence in the literature that ambiguity — not\n765\t    surface variation — drives safety violations. The measurement contribution stands\n766\t    independent of failure mode taxonomy validation.'''\n767\t- name: sopbench_cross_benchmark\n768\t  plan_id: plan_017\n769\t  description: C1 replication on SOPBench Healthcare + Bank. 100 clause pairs × 2\n770\t    conditions × 5 models = 1,000 episodes.\n771\t  expected_results:\n772\t    total_episodes: 987\n773\t    episodes_dropped: 13\n774\t    c1_delta_pp_overall: 14.2\n775\t    c1_delta_gpt54: 18.4\n776\t    c1_delta_gpt41: 16.8\n777\t    c1_delta_deepseek: 15.0\n778\t    c1_delta_qwen3: 12.6\n779\t    c1_delta_claude: 8.2\n780\t    c1_p_gpt54: 0.003\n781\t    c1_p_gpt41: 0.008\n782\t    c1_p_deepseek: 0.015\n783\t    c1_p_qwen3: 0.061\n784\t    c1_p_claude: 0.093\n785\t    models_c1_significant: 3/5\n786\t    models_c1_nonsignificant: 2/5\n787\t    c2_type_rank_rho: 0.2\n788\t    c2_type_rank_p: 0.8\n789\t    healthcare_delta_pp: 16.1\n790\t    bank_delta_pp: 12.3\n791\t  result_rationale: 'SOPBench C1 replication is WEAK — a defining feature of worst\n792\t    tier. Overall Δ=14.2pp is less than half the τ²-bench effect (+34pp). Only 3/5\n793\t    models reach significance; Claude (Δ=8.2pp, p=0.093) and Qwen3 (Δ=12.6pp, p=0.061)\n794\t    fall short. Type ranking ρ=0.20 (p=0.80) shows NO correspondence with τ²-bench\n795\t    hierarchy. Multiple reinforcing causes: (1) SOPBench''s rule-based verifier catches\n796\t    explicit rule violations only, missing nuanced safety violations that τ²-bench''s\n797\t    LLM judge detects — ambiguity causes agents to act inappropriately without technically\n798\t    breaking any explicit SOPBench rule; (2) Healthcare/Bank SOPs are more procedurally\n799\t    structured (if-then-else decision trees) than retail/airline policies, reducing\n800\t    ambiguity surface area; (3) 100 clause pairs across 4 types give ~25 per type\n801\t    per domain — insufficient for stable per-type estimates; (4) type rankings depend\n802\t    on domain structure: auth_scope is more dangerous in banking (authorization is\n803\t    core) than in τ²-bench retail, reshuffling the hierarchy. The weak replication\n804\t    doesn''t invalidate τ²-bench but prevents cross-domain generalizability claims.'\n805\t  tables:\n806\t  - title: SOPBench C1 Replication by Model\n807\t    columns:\n808\t    - Model\n809\t    - Amb Rate\n810\t    - Unamb Rate\n811\t    - Delta pp\n812\t    - p-value\n813\t    - Replicates?\n814\t    rows:\n815\t    - - gpt-5.4\n816\t      - '0.312'\n817\t      - '0.128'\n818\t      - '+18.4'\n819\t      - '0.003'\n820\t      - 'Yes'\n821\t    - - gpt-4.1\n822\t      - '0.288'\n823\t      - '0.120'\n824\t      - '+16.8'\n825\t      - '0.008'\n826\t      - 'Yes'\n827\t    - - deepseek\n828\t      - '0.298'\n829\t      - '0.148'\n830\t      - '+15.0'\n831\t      - '0.015'\n832\t      - 'Yes'\n833\t    - - qwen3\n834\t      - '0.262'\n835\t      - '0.136'\n836\t      - '+12.6'\n837\t      - '0.061'\n838\t      - 'No'\n839\t    - - claude\n840\t      - '0.194'\n841\t      - '0.112'\n842\t      - '+8.2'\n843\t      - '0.093'\n844\t      - 'No'\n845\t  - title: SOPBench vs tau2-bench Type Ranking (4 shared types)\n846\t    columns:\n847\t    - tau2-bench Rank\n848\t    - tau2-bench Type\n849\t    - SOPBench Rank\n850\t    - SOPBench Type\n851\t    rows:\n852\t    - - '1'\n853\t      - incompleteness (53.6%)\n854\t      - '1'\n855\t      - auth_scope (31.2%)\n856\t    - - '2'\n857\t      - lexical (42.8%)\n858\t      - '2'\n859\t      - incompleteness (28.4%)\n860\t    - - '3'\n861\t      - auth_scope (41.6%)\n862\t      - '3'\n863\t      - lexical (25.1%)\n864\t    - - '4'\n865\t      - cond_prec (36.8%)\n866\t      - '4'\n867\t      - cond_prec (21.1%)\n868\t  - title: SOPBench by Domain\n869\t    columns:\n870\t    - Domain\n871\t    - Amb Rate\n872\t    - Unamb Rate\n873\t    - Delta pp\n874\t    - p-value\n875\t    rows:\n876\t    - - Healthcare\n877\t      - '0.289'\n878\t      - '0.128'\n879\t      - '+16.1'\n880\t      - '0.004'\n881\t    - - Bank\n882\t      - '0.253'\n883\t      - '0.130'\n884\t      - '+12.3'\n885\t      - '0.031'\n886\t  figures_description:\n887\t  - 'Side-by-side forest plots: τ²-bench (left, tight CIs far from zero) vs SOPBench\n888\t    (right, wider CIs, 2 overlapping zero). Visual contrast makes attenuation immediately\n889\t    apparent.'\n890\t  analysis: 'SOPBench weakness forces a major reframing. Under worst tier, the paper\n891\t    drops all cross-domain generalizability claims. SOPBench results go in appendix:\n892\t    ''Effect replicates directionally in 3/5 models on SOPBench (Healthcare + Bank)\n893\t    but with substantially attenuated magnitude (14.2pp vs 34.0pp) and inconsistent\n894\t    type ranking (ρ=0.20, n.s.), suggesting effect size depends on policy complexity\n895\t    and evaluation methodology. We frame τ²-bench scope (retail + airline) as an acknowledged\n896\t    limitation.'' The rebuttal argument: ''Attenuation is expected when moving from\n897\t    LLM judge to rule-based verifier — the verifier captures a strict subset of violations.\n898\t    The directional replication in 3/5 models is consistent with a real but domain-modulated\n899\t    effect.'''\n900\t- name: classifier_poc\n901\t  plan_id: plan_008\n902\t  description: DeBERTa-v3-large binary ambiguity detector, fine-tuned on 300 clause\n903\t    pairs, 5-fold CV.\n904\t  expected_results:\n905\t    macro_f1: 0.55\n906\t    binary_f1: 0.78\n907\t    binary_fpr: 0.062\n908\t    best_type_f1_cp: 0.89\n909\t    worst_type_f1_incomp: 0.28\n910\t    per_type_f1_cp: 0.89\n911\t    per_type_f1_scopal: 0.71\n912\t    per_type_f1_coref: 0.62\n913\t    per_type_f1_auth: 0.51\n914\t    per_type_f1_lexical: 0.34\n915\t    per_type_f1_incomp: 0.28\n916\t  result_rationale: 'Binary detection (F1=0.78) is serviceable but per-type classification\n917\t    (macro F1=0.55) is mediocre. CP is easiest (explicit conflicting rules have strong\n918\t    lexical markers — ''however,'' ''notwithstanding''). Incompleteness is hardest\n919\t    (absence of information has no positive signal — the classifier must learn what''s\n920\t    MISSING, a fundamentally harder task). Under worst tier, the classifier''s type-level\n921\t    struggle mirrors the κ re-annotation failure — both reflect that fine-grained\n922\t    type distinctions are harder than binary ambiguity detection. The classifier is\n923\t    relegated to appendix: ''Preliminary DeBERTa PoC achieves F1=0.78 for binary ambiguity\n924\t    screening. Per-type classification (F1=0.55) remains challenging, consistent with\n925\t    type boundary issues identified in our annotation study.'''\n926\t  tables:\n927\t  - title: DeBERTa Classifier Per-Type F1\n928\t    columns:\n929\t    - Type\n930\t    - Precision\n931\t    - Recall\n932\t    - F1\n933\t    rows:\n934\t    - - conditional_precedence\n935\t      - '0.91'\n936\t      - '0.87'\n937\t      - '0.89'\n938\t    - - scopal\n939\t      - '0.74'\n940\t      - '0.68'\n941\t      - '0.71'\n942\t    - - coreferential\n943\t      - '0.65'\n944\t      - '0.59'\n945\t      - '0.62'\n946\t    - - authorization_scope\n947\t      - '0.54'\n948\t      - '0.48'\n949\t      - '0.51'\n950\t    - - lexical\n951\t      - '0.38'\n952\t      - '0.31'\n953\t      - '0.34'\n954\t    - - incompleteness\n955\t      - '0.32'\n956\t      - '0.25'\n957\t      - '0.28'\n958\t    - - Binary (any type)\n959\t      - '0.82'\n960\t      - '0.74'\n961\t      - '0.78'\n962\t  figures_description:\n963\t  - '6×6 confusion matrix: strong diagonal for CP/scopal, diffuse off-diagonal for\n964\t    incompleteness/lexical — auth_scope confused with incompleteness in ~25% of cases.'\n965\t  analysis: Classifier PoC is P2 and doesn't affect paper positioning. Under worst\n966\t    tier, it's an appendix footnote. Its per-type difficulty gradient (CP easy → incomp\n967\t    hard) independently corroborates that type boundaries are genuinely fuzzy, lending\n968\t    credibility to the 'honest assessment' of taxonomy limitations.\n969\t- name: kappa_reannotation_failure\n970\t  plan_id: what_if_kappa_reannotation\n971\t  description: Human re-annotation of 25 LLM-coded failure mode traces using improved\n972\t    codebook. THE defining worst-tier experiment.\n973\t  expected_results:\n974\t    n_traces_reannotated: 25\n975\t    kappa_human_human: 0.48\n976\t    kappa_human_llm_pooled: 0.22\n977\t    human_llm_dominant_mode_agreement: 2/6\n978\t    types_with_agreement:\n979\t    - scopal\n980\t    - conditional_precedence\n981\t    types_with_disagreement:\n982\t    - auth_scope\n983\t    - incompleteness\n984\t    - lexical\n985\t    - coreferential\n986\t    auth_scope_human_dominant: scope_misapplication\n987\t    auth_scope_llm_dominant: unauthorized_escalation\n988\t    incompleteness_human_dominant: inappropriate_action\n989\t    incompleteness_llm_dominant: assumption_based_action\n990\t    systematic_llm_bias_types: 2\n991\t  result_rationale: 'κ=0.22 (poor) exposes two distinct and reinforcing problems.\n992\t    First, LLM coding bias: LLM coders systematically miscategorize by surface keyword\n993\t    matching. For auth_scope, traces containing ''exceeded authority'' or ''unauthorized''\n994\t    are coded as unauthorized_escalation regardless of whether the agent actively\n995\t    chose to exceed boundaries (true escalation) or passively misread the permission\n996\t    scope (scope misapplication). Human coders, reading the full reasoning chain,\n997\t    find ~40% of auth_scope ''unauthorized_escalation'' traces are actually scope_misapplication\n998\t    — the agent didn''t know it was exceeding authority. For incompleteness, LLMs\n999\t    code ''assumption_based_action'' when the trace shows gap-filling behavior, but\n1000\t    humans often judge these as ''inappropriate_action'' — the distinction between\n1001\t    ''filling a gap'' and ''acting wrongly'' is interpretive, and humans apply stricter\n1002\t    criteria. Second, codebook imprecision: human-human κ=0.48 (moderate) reveals\n1003\t    that the coding scheme''s category boundaries are inherently fuzzy. The improved\n1004\t    codebook (collapsed spurious-compliance/structural-misparse) was insufficient\n1005\t    — the remaining categories (unauthorized_escalation ↔ scope_misapplication, assumption_based_action\n1006\t    ↔ inappropriate_action) still overlap when applied to real traces. Only scopal\n1007\t    (clear scope_misapplication with unambiguous behavioral signature) and CP (explicit\n1008\t    rule conflict → arbitrary_rule_selection with detectably different pattern) survive\n1009\t    because their failure modes have distinct, objectively identifiable behavioral\n1010\t    markers. The LLM-LLM κ=0.52 from plan_005 was artificially inflated by shared\n1011\t    biases — both coders use the same keyword heuristic, producing correlated errors\n1012\t    that inflate agreement. The root cause is that agent trace text doesn''t contain\n1013\t    sufficient information to distinguish between failure mechanisms (intent-level\n1014\t    distinction) — you''d need the agent''s internal chain-of-thought, which is only\n1015\t    partially revealed.'\n1016\t  tables:\n1017\t  - title: Human vs LLM Dominant Failure Mode by Type\n1018\t    columns:\n1019\t    - Type\n1020\t    - LLM Dominant\n1021\t    - LLM %\n1022\t    - Human Dominant\n1023\t    - Human %\n1024\t    - Agree?\n1025\t    rows:\n1026\t    - - auth_scope\n1027\t      - unauthorized_escalation\n1028\t      - 78%\n1029\t      - scope_misapplication\n1030\t      - 52%\n1031\t      - 'No'\n1032\t    - - incompleteness\n1033\t      - assumption_based_action\n1034\t      - 64%\n1035\t      - inappropriate_action\n1036\t      - 45%\n1037\t      - 'No'\n1038\t    - - scopal\n1039\t      - scope_misapplication\n1040\t      - 75%\n1041\t      - scope_misapplication\n1042\t      - 68%\n1043\t      - 'Yes'\n1044\t    - - lexical\n1045\t      - assumption_based_action\n1046\t      - 62%\n1047\t      - assumption_based_action\n1048\t      - 41%\n1049\t      - 'No'\n1050\t    - - cond_prec\n1051\t      - arbitrary_rule_selection\n1052\t      - 34%\n1053\t      - arbitrary_rule_selection\n1054\t      - 42%\n1055\t      - 'Yes'\n1056\t    - - coreferential\n1057\t      - scope_misapplication\n1058\t      - 41%\n1059\t      - assumption_based_action\n1060\t      - 36%\n1061\t      - 'No'\n1062\t  - title: kappa Agreement Summary\n1063\t    columns:\n1064\t    - Comparison\n1065\t    - Overall kappa\n1066\t    - Interpretation\n1067\t    rows:\n1068\t    - - Human-Human\n1069\t      - '0.48'\n1070\t      - Moderate — codebook boundaries inherently fuzzy\n1071\t    - - Human-LLM (pooled)\n1072\t      - '0.22'\n1073\t      - Poor — systematic LLM keyword bias\n1074\t    - - LLM-LLM (plan_005)\n1075\t      - '0.52'\n1076\t      - Moderate — inflated by shared bias\n1077\t  - title: Systematic LLM Coding Biases Identified\n1078\t    columns:\n1079\t    - Type\n1080\t    - LLM Bias Pattern\n1081\t    - Human Finding\n1082\t    - Impact\n1083\t    rows:\n1084\t    - - auth_scope\n1085\t      - keyword 'unauthorized' -> unauthorized_escalation\n1086\t      - 40% are scope_misapplication\n1087\t      - C5 auth_scope mechanism claim invalid\n1088\t    - - incompleteness\n1089\t      - keyword 'assumed' -> assumption_based_action\n1090\t      - 30% are inappropriate_action\n1091\t      - C5 incompleteness mechanism weakened\n1092\t    - - lexical\n1093\t      - agreement inflated by kappa paradox\n1094\t      - true diversity higher than coded\n1095\t      - fine-grained % unreliable\n1096\t    - - coreferential\n1097\t      - coded as scope_misapplication plurality\n1098\t      - flat distribution, no clear dominant\n1099\t      - C5 coref pattern overclaimed\n1100\t  figures_description:\n1101\t  - 'Confusion matrix (4×4): human-coded (rows) vs LLM-coded (columns). Dense off-diagonal\n1102\t    cluster at unauthorized_escalation(LLM) × scope_misapplication(Human) for auth_scope\n1103\t    traces — the single largest disagreement cell.'\n1104\t  - 'Paired bar chart: LLM-coded vs human-coded failure mode distributions per type.\n1105\t    Scopal and CP bars match; auth_scope and incompleteness bars diverge sharply.'\n1106\t  analysis: 'κ=0.22 is the worst-tier trigger that cascades through the paper. Consequences:\n1107\t    (1) C5 failure mode taxonomy is invalidated for fine-grained claims — must be\n1108\t    demoted to appendix as ''exploratory automated analysis.'' (2) ''Ambiguity Blindness''\n1109\t    loses named-contribution status but survives as a Discussion paragraph (binary\n1110\t    observation, not dependent on failure mode coding). (3) Contribution (3) downgrades\n1111\t    from ''type-specific failure mode taxonomy'' to ''benchmark and protocol release.''\n1112\t    (4) Discussion shifts from mechanism (''why models fail differently'') to implication\n1113\t    (''what the differential vulnerability means for deployment''). (5) The paper''s\n1114\t    two remaining validated contributions are C1 causal quantification and C4 model×type\n1115\t    interaction — both purely quantitative. The human-human κ=0.48 provides a partial\n1116\t    defense: ''The failure mode categories themselves have inherently fuzzy boundaries;\n1117\t    this is a limitation of trace-based mechanism inference, not just LLM coding quality.\n1118\t    Future work with chain-of-thought access may enable more reliable mechanism classification.''\n1119\t    Expected reviewer reaction: ''The authors are commendably honest about this limitation,\n1120\t    but contribution (3) as stated in the abstract is not supported by the validation\n1121\t    data.'''\n1122\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b364c86e-c33c-4dfc-add1-ad49dc9f2992","uuid":"ca2ec26c-c812-40c2-8998-9bae8058e875","timestamp":"2026-05-23T20:00:04.562Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-4/projects/policy_ambiguity_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260523T185344/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'κ re-annotation (κ=0.22) exposes LLM failure-mode coding as surface-keyword\n  heuristic, invalidating C5 fine-grained claims. SOPBench C1 effect attenuates to\n  ~14pp with 2/5 models non-significant, blocking cross-domain generalizability. Paper\n  reframes as measurement/benchmarking contribution: (1) causal quantification (+34pp,\n  OR=15.1, perturbation-controlled), (2) model×type interaction (52pp auth_scope range),\n  (3) benchmark+protocol release (2,997 episodes + matched-pair design). C5 failure\n  mode taxonomy demoted to appendix (''exploratory automated analysis''); Ambiguity\n  Blindness retained as one-paragraph Discussion observation. Title pivots to ''Measuring\n  Differential Vulnerability to Policy Ambiguity Across LLM Agent Families.'' Discussion\n  shifts from ''why models fail differently'' to ''implications for model selection\n  and deployment risk.'' Expected ARR 3.0–3.49 — ''well-executed empirical infrastructure,\n  incremental theoretical contribution.'''\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: full_study_clause_generation\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: Generate 140 new clauses, validate all 300 pairs for cross-type balance\n    and quality.\n  expected_results:\n    clauses_generated: 140\n    total_validated: 300\n    quality_pass_rate: 0.93\n    boundary_overlap_cases: 8\n    mean_disambiguation_tokens_spec: 35.2\n    mean_disambiguation_tokens_ling: 18.4\n    revision_needed: 21\n  result_rationale: Clause generation is upstream of worst-tier failures and proceeds\n    normally. 93% pass quality check. 8 clauses sit on type boundaries (5 auth_scope↔incompleteness,\n    3 coreferential↔lexical) — individually acceptable, but these boundary cases seed\n    downstream κ re-annotation disagreement when human coders classify 4/8 into different\n    types. Spec-layer disambiguation requires ~35 tokens vs ling-layer ~18, a systematic\n    asymmetry inherent to matched-pair design that plan_007 will flag.\n  tables:\n  - title: Clause Generation Summary by Type\n    columns:\n    - Type\n    - Existing\n    - New\n    - Total\n    - Pass\n    - Boundary\n    rows:\n    - - authorization_scope\n      - '0'\n      - '50'\n      - '50'\n      - '46'\n      - '3'\n    - - conditional_precedence\n      - '50'\n      - '0'\n      - '50'\n      - '49'\n      - '0'\n    - - incompleteness\n      - '30'\n      - '20'\n      - '50'\n      - '47'\n      - '2'\n    - - lexical\n      - '30'\n      - '20'\n      - '50'\n      - '48'\n      - '2'\n    - - coreferential\n      - '0'\n      - '50'\n      - '50'\n      - '45'\n      - '1'\n    - - scopal\n      - '50'\n      - '0'\n      - '50'\n      - '49'\n      - '0'\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: Clause generation succeeds. 8/300 (2.7%) boundary overlap is within tolerance,\n    but under worst tier these become evidence of taxonomy imprecision when combined\n    with κ=0.22 re-annotation failure. Token-length asymmetry (35 vs 18 tokens) contributes\n    to plan_007's 2/4 significant KS tests.\n- name: new_type_mini_pilot\n  plan_id: plan_001b\n  description: 'Mini-pilot: 5 clauses/type × 2 conditions × 1 model (gpt-5.4) = 20\n    episodes for coreferential + auth_scope.'\n  expected_results:\n    total_episodes: 20\n    auth_scope_amb_violation_rate: 0.4\n    auth_scope_unamb_violation_rate: 0.1\n    coref_amb_violation_rate: 0.2\n    coref_unamb_violation_rate: 0.0\n    pilot_verdict: PASS\n  result_rationale: Both types trigger violations under ambiguity, clearing pilot\n    thresholds. Auth_scope at 40% (2/5) seems strong; coreferential at 20% (1/5) is\n    low but non-zero. N=5 per type is designed to catch gross failures only — it cannot\n    detect the failure mode coding problems that surface at scale. In hindsight, the\n    pilot validated binary effects but left untested the question of whether auth_scope's\n    failure modes are reliably classifiable, which is where worst tier ultimately\n    collapses.\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: Pilot passes and green-lights full study. Under worst tier, its limited\n    N is a non-factor — the pilot's purpose (binary effect validation) is orthogonal\n    to the failure mode coding problem that drives worst-tier collapse.\n- name: full_study_api\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 300 clauses × 2 conditions × 3 API models = 1,800 episodes with cross-judge\n    evaluation.\n  expected_results:\n    total_episodes: 1800\n    c1_delta_pp_aggregate: 34.0\n    c1_mcnemar_or: 15.1\n    c1_or_ci:\n    - 10.78\n    - 21.17\n    c1_p: <0.0001\n    c1_delta_gpt54: 38.3\n    c1_delta_gpt41: 34.7\n    c1_delta_claude: 25.7\n    cross_judge_kappa: 0.69\n    amb_violation_rate_pooled: 0.41\n    unamb_violation_rate_pooled: 0.045\n  result_rationale: 'Data collection succeeds. Binary violation effect is massive\n    across all 3 API models with no exceptions. Cross-judge κ=0.69 (substantial) validates\n    the violation measurement chain. These results are tier-invariant — the +34pp\n    effect and OR=15.1 hold regardless of downstream κ re-annotation or SOPBench outcomes.\n    Under worst tier, these numbers become the paper''s anchor: quantitatively unassailable,\n    but without C5 qualitative story they constitute a measurement finding rather\n    than a theoretical contribution.'\n  tables:\n  - title: C1 Binary Effect by API Model\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Amb Rate\n    - Unamb Rate\n    - Delta pp\n    - McNemar p\n    - Judge\n    rows:\n    - - gpt-5.4\n      - '0.410'\n      - '0.027'\n      - '+38.3'\n      - <1e-17\n      - gpt-4.1\n    - - gpt-4.1\n      - '0.407'\n      - '0.060'\n      - '+34.7'\n      - <1e-17\n      - gpt-5.4\n    - - claude-sonnet-4-6\n      - '0.303'\n      - '0.047'\n      - '+25.7'\n      - <1e-17\n      - gpt-5.4\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Forest plot: per-model binary effect sizes with 95% CI — all far from zero, Claude\n    lower but still massive.'\n  analysis: C1 is the paper's unassailable result in all tiers. +34pp with perturbation\n    control is the strongest causal evidence for ambiguity→safety violations in the\n    literature. Under worst tier, this anchors the reframed 'measurement paper' positioning.\n- name: full_study_opensource\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 300 clauses × 2 conditions × 2 open-source models (qwen3-235b, deepseek-v3)\n    via OpenRouter = 1,200 episodes.\n  expected_results:\n    total_episodes: 1197\n    c1_delta_qwen3: 32.4\n    c1_delta_deepseek: 39.0\n    qwen3_amb_rate: 0.431\n    deepseek_amb_rate: 0.497\n    deepseek_type_chi2: 6.31\n    deepseek_type_p: 0.277\n    deepseek_type_v: 0.14\n    qwen3_type_chi2: 15.89\n    qwen3_type_p: 0.007\n    api_timeouts: 3\n  result_rationale: 'Both open-source models confirm C1 universality. DeepSeek shows\n    the highest overall vulnerability (49.7%) with near-uniform rates across all 6\n    types (χ²=6.31, p=0.277) — no type differentiation. This uniformity means DeepSeek\n    doesn''t contribute to C4''s model×type interaction; it has no type-specific vulnerability\n    profile. Under worst tier, DeepSeek''s flat profile feeds the ''incremental''\n    narrative: type differentiation may be an artifact of targeted safety training\n    (GPT, Claude) rather than a fundamental property of ambiguity types. Qwen3 shows\n    moderate differentiation (χ²=15.89, p=0.007) partially rescuing C4 as cross-architecture.'\n  tables:\n  - title: Open-Source Violation Rates by Type (Ambiguous)\n    columns:\n    - Type\n    - Qwen3\n    - DeepSeek\n    - Range\n    rows:\n    - - authorization_scope\n      - 26%\n      - 60%\n      - 34pp\n    - - conditional_precedence\n      - 40%\n      - 38%\n      - 2pp\n    - - incompleteness\n      - 62%\n      - 54%\n      - 8pp\n    - - lexical\n      - 52%\n      - 54%\n      - 2pp\n    - - coreferential\n      - 42%\n      - 44%\n      - 2pp\n    - - scopal\n      - 37%\n      - 48%\n      - 11pp\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'DeepSeek''s uniformly high vulnerability (no type χ² significance) weakens\n    C4 by showing 1/5 models has no type-specific profile. Under worst tier, a reviewer\n    argues: ''The model×type interaction is driven by 3 models with targeted safety\n    training — it reflects training decisions, not an intrinsic property of ambiguity\n    type.'''\n- name: full_study_analysis\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Statistical analysis of 2,997 episodes: C1–C4, TOST, power, info delta,\n    inter-model agreement.'\n  expected_results:\n    c1_or: 15.1\n    c1_or_ci:\n    - 10.78\n    - 21.17\n    c1_bootstrap_ci_pp:\n    - 31.2\n    - 36.9\n    c2_omnibus_chi2: 28.02\n    c2_p: 3.6e-05\n    c2_v: 0.137\n    c2_power: 0.993\n    c2_mde: 0.093\n    c3_delta_pp: 6.08\n    c3_p_aggregate: 0.017\n    c3_within_judge_gpt54_p: 0.059\n    c3_within_judge_gpt41_p: 0.157\n    c4_model_chi2: 31.02\n    c4_model_p: 3.0e-06\n    c4_model_v: 0.102\n    tost_15pp_equivalent: 8/15\n    tost_10pp_equivalent: 4/15\n    logistic_condition_coef: 2.278\n    logistic_condition_z: 19.65\n    logistic_token_delta_coef: 0.022\n    logistic_token_delta_p: 0.0005\n    fleiss_kappa_inter_model: 0.384\n    systematic_nocuity_rate: 0.27\n  result_rationale: 'All quantitative results are tier-invariant. C1 is overwhelming\n    (OR=15.1). C4''s model×type interaction is statistically significant and novel.\n    However, under worst tier reviewers evaluate these numbers without C5''s explanatory\n    scaffolding. V=0.137 for C2 falls below the conventional 0.20 medium-effect threshold\n    — invites ''small effect'' criticism. C3 dissolves under within-judge analysis\n    (gpt-5.4 group p=0.059, gpt-4.1 group p=0.157): pooled significance may reflect\n    between-judge variance, not a real layer effect. TOST shows 4/15 type pairs equivalent\n    at ±10pp — types ARE differentiable but the differences are modest. A skeptical\n    reviewer sees: ''confirmed the obvious (ambiguity is bad), found a small type\n    effect (V=0.137), showed models differ — well-executed but incremental.'' The\n    logistic regression''s condition_amb coef=2.278 dominating all predictors is strong\n    evidence but ''expected, not surprising.'' Systematic nocuity (27% of episodes:\n    all models converge on same wrong interpretation) is theoretically interesting\n    but requires C5 to explain WHY.'\n  tables:\n  - title: C2 Type Hierarchy (Ambiguous Only, 5-Model Pooled)\n    columns:\n    - Type\n    - Violation Rate\n    - 95% CI\n    - Tier\n    rows:\n    - - incompleteness\n      - 53.6%\n      - '[48.2, 59.0]'\n      - High\n    - - lexical\n      - 42.8%\n      - '[37.4, 48.2]'\n      - Mid\n    - - authorization_scope\n      - 41.6%\n      - '[36.2, 47.0]'\n      - Mid\n    - - scopal\n      - 39.4%\n      - '[34.1, 44.7]'\n      - Mid\n    - - conditional_precedence\n      - 36.8%\n      - '[31.5, 42.1]'\n      - Mid\n    - - coreferential\n      - 31.6%\n      - '[26.5, 36.7]'\n      - Low\n  - title: C3 Layer Effect — Within-Judge Fragility\n    columns:\n    - Judge Group\n    - Spec Rate\n    - Ling Rate\n    - Delta pp\n    - p-value\n    - Verdict\n    rows:\n    - - Pooled\n      - 44.0%\n      - 37.9%\n      - '+6.08'\n      - '0.017'\n      - Significant\n    - - gpt-4.1 judged\n      - 48.4%\n      - 40.4%\n      - '+8.0'\n      - '0.059'\n      - Marginal\n    - - gpt-5.4 judged\n      - 41.0%\n      - 36.2%\n      - '+4.8'\n      - '0.157'\n      - Not significant\n  - title: C4 Model × Type Interaction (Violation Rates, Ambiguous)\n    columns:\n    - Type\n    - GPT-5.4\n    - GPT-4.1\n    - Claude\n    - Qwen3\n    - DeepSeek\n    - Range\n    rows:\n    - - auth_scope\n      - 64%\n      - 46%\n      - 12%\n      - 26%\n      - 60%\n      - 52pp\n    - - incompleteness\n      - 46%\n      - 58%\n      - 48%\n      - 62%\n      - 54%\n      - 16pp\n    - - lexical\n      - 42%\n      - 34%\n      - 32%\n      - 52%\n      - 54%\n      - 22pp\n    - - coreferential\n      - 18%\n      - 32%\n      - 22%\n      - 42%\n      - 44%\n      - 26pp\n    - - scopal\n      - 42%\n      - 38%\n      - 32%\n      - 37%\n      - 48%\n      - 16pp\n    - - cond_prec\n      - 34%\n      - 36%\n      - 36%\n      - 40%\n      - 38%\n      - 6pp\n  figures_description:\n  - 5×6 heatmap with diverging color scale (10% green → 65% red). Auth_scope row shows\n    dramatic gradient; incompleteness row uniformly hot; cond_prec row near-uniform\n    mid-range.\n  - 'Forest plot: per-type binary effects with 95% CI. All above zero, incompleteness\n    CI highest and narrowest.'\n  analysis: 'The quantitative core is rock-solid but lacks interpretive depth without\n    C5. Under worst tier, the ''numbers without narrative'' problem is acute: C2''s\n    V=0.137 is significant but small, C3 is fragile, and C4''s 52pp auth_scope range\n    — while striking — is concentrated in one type. The 27% systematic nocuity finding\n    (all models converge on same wrong answer) would be the Discussion centerpiece\n    IF C5 could explain WHY, but without validated failure modes it remains a descriptive\n    observation. Reviewers see a comprehensive measurement study that stops short\n    of mechanistic explanation.'\n- name: error_analysis\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Qualitative analysis of violation traces: failure mode classification,\n    cross-type distribution, ambiguity awareness.'\n  expected_results:\n    traces_coded_gpt54: 123\n    traces_coded_gpt41: 122\n    total_violations_coded: 614\n    inter_coder_kappa_4cat: 0.517\n    percent_agreement: 0.656\n    types_unanimous_dominant_fm: 3/6\n    types_majority_dominant_fm: 6/6\n    disambiguation_awareness_gpt54: 0.041\n    disambiguation_awareness_gpt41: 0.107\n    surface_adoption_overall: 0.88\n    escalate_to_user_rate: 0.008\n    auth_scope_inter_coder_kappa: 0.054\n    incompleteness_inter_coder_kappa: 0.233\n    lexical_inter_coder_kappa: 0.0\n    scopal_percent_agreement: 1.0\n  result_rationale: 'LLM-coded error analysis produces an internally consistent but\n    artificially clean picture. Both GPT coders share similar linguistic biases from\n    pretraining, making their agreement (3/6 dominant mode, κ=0.52) an artifact of\n    shared surface-feature heuristics rather than robust coding. Auth_scope κ=0.054\n    (near chance) because both coders assign >80% unauthorized_escalation — they agree\n    on the label but disagree on specific traces, and human re-annotation will show\n    the label itself is wrong for ~40% of cases (should be scope_misapplication).\n    Lexical κ=0.0 is a kappa paradox (91.7% agreement but chance-corrected near zero).\n    Ambiguity Blindness (4.1–10.7%) survives worst tier because it''s based on text\n    search for explicit acknowledgment — a binary signal immune to failure mode coding\n    bias. Surface adoption >88% is similarly robust. The key worst-tier dynamic: plan_005\n    results LOOK usable until kappa re-annotation reveals the LLM-LLM agreement is\n    systematically biased.'\n  tables:\n  - title: LLM-Coded Dominant Failure Mode by Type\n    columns:\n    - Type\n    - GPT-5.4 Dominant\n    - Rate\n    - GPT-4.1 Dominant\n    - Rate\n    - Agree?\n    rows:\n    - - auth_scope\n      - unauthorized_escalation\n      - 72%\n      - unauthorized_escalation\n      - 83%\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - incompleteness\n      - assumption_based_action\n      - 61%\n      - assumption_based_action\n      - 66%\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - scopal\n      - scope_misapplication\n      - 76%\n      - scope_misapplication\n      - 74%\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - lexical\n      - assumption_based_action\n      - 71%\n      - assumption_based_action\n      - 53%\n      - Partial\n    - - cond_prec\n      - scope_misapplication\n      - 41%\n      - arbitrary_rule_selection\n      - 28%\n      - 'No'\n    - - coreferential\n      - scope_misapplication\n      - 44%\n      - assumption_based_action\n      - 38%\n      - 'No'\n  - title: Per-Type Inter-Coder Agreement\n    columns:\n    - Type\n    - N Traces\n    - '% Agree'\n    - Cohen kappa\n    - Issue\n    rows:\n    - - auth_scope\n      - '42'\n      - 54%\n      - '0.054'\n      - keyword-driven label, not mechanism\n    - - incompleteness\n      - '56'\n      - 62%\n      - '0.233'\n      - fuzzy boundary with inappropriate_action\n    - - lexical\n      - '38'\n      - 92%\n      - '0.000'\n      - kappa paradox\n    - - scopal\n      - '36'\n      - 100%\n      - undefined\n      - perfect agreement, undefined kappa\n    - - cond_prec\n      - '42'\n      - 58%\n      - '0.312'\n      - scope_misapplication vs arbitrary_rule_selection\n    - - coreferential\n      - '31'\n      - 48%\n      - '0.184'\n      - flat distribution, no dominant mode\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Stacked bar chart per type: failure mode distribution. Auth_scope and scopal\n    show sharp single-mode dominance; cond_prec, coreferential show flat multi-mode\n    distributions.'\n  analysis: Error analysis is the CRITICAL worst-tier failure surface. The LLM-coded\n    results appear publishable in isolation (6/6 majority agreement, Ambiguity Blindness\n    quantified, layer mechanism documented). But κ re-annotation will expose that\n    the type-specific failure signatures — the core of C5 — are an artifact of LLM\n    keyword matching. The unauthorized_escalation label for auth_scope reflects the\n    coders detecting 'agent exceeded authority' rather than distinguishing HOW (active\n    escalation vs passive misread). Ambiguity Blindness (<5-11%) and surface adoption\n    (>88%) survive because they don't require fine-grained mode classification. Under\n    worst-tier reframing, these two binary findings move to Discussion; the rest of\n    C5 moves to appendix with 'automated coding, κ=0.22 with human re-annotation'\n    caveat.\n- name: reliability_test\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Test-retest: 30 clauses × 3 replicates × 2 conditions × 2 models =\n    360 episodes. ICC for per-clause violation rates.'\n  expected_results:\n    total_episodes: 360\n    icc_overall: 0.919\n    icc_binary: 0.847\n    icc_gpt54: 0.921\n    icc_gpt41: 0.918\n    icc_scopal: 0.989\n    icc_lexical: 0.981\n    icc_coref: 0.976\n    icc_auth_scope: 0.857\n    icc_cond_prec: 0.856\n    icc_incompleteness: 0.807\n  result_rationale: 'Test-retest measures violation rate reproducibility, not failure\n    mode coding — so it''s tier-invariant. ICC=0.919 (excellent) confirms per-clause\n    rates are stable across independent runs. The spec/ling ICC pattern (ling 0.976–0.989\n    vs spec 0.807–0.857) reflects real behavioral variance: spec-layer types force\n    agents to guess without disambiguation channels, producing less deterministic\n    outcomes on repeat. This finding actually SUPPORTS the Disambiguation Channel\n    Framework, but without C5 validation it remains purely descriptive. Under worst\n    tier, the per-type ICC variability adds nuance to C3 fragility — spec-layer types\n    are both more dangerous AND less reproducible.'\n  tables:\n  - title: ICC by Type and Layer\n    columns:\n    - Type\n    - Layer\n    - ICC\n    - 95% CI\n    rows:\n    - - scopal\n      - Linguistic\n      - '0.989'\n      - '[0.97, 0.99]'\n    - - lexical\n      - Linguistic\n      - '0.981'\n      - '[0.96, 0.99]'\n    - - coreferential\n      - Linguistic\n      - '0.976'\n      - '[0.95, 0.99]'\n    - - auth_scope\n      - Specification\n      - '0.857'\n      - '[0.74, 0.93]'\n    - - cond_prec\n      - Specification\n      - '0.856'\n      - '[0.74, 0.93]'\n    - - incompleteness\n      - Specification\n      - '0.807'\n      - '[0.67, 0.90]'\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'Reliability is a bright spot — ICC=0.919 means the measurement infrastructure\n    is sound. Under worst tier, this partially offsets the C5 collapse: violation\n    RATES are reliable even if failure MODE labels are not. The spec/ling ICC gap\n    (0.83 vs 0.98 mean) is convergent evidence for the Disambiguation Channel Framework\n    but remains observational without C5 mechanism.'\n- name: realism_validation\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: KS tests comparing synthetic clauses vs DRIP-R natural ambiguities\n    on 4 linguistic properties.\n  expected_results:\n    ks_sentence_length_p: 0.028\n    ks_flesch_kincaid_p: 0.112\n    ks_parse_depth_p: 0.039\n    ks_ttr_p: 0.234\n    significant_metrics: 2/4\n    mean_length_synthetic: 31.4\n    mean_length_drip_r: 26.1\n    mean_parse_depth_synthetic: 4.8\n    mean_parse_depth_drip_r: 4.1\n  result_rationale: '2/4 KS tests reject null: synthetic clauses are ~5 tokens longer\n    (31.4 vs 26.1) and ~0.7 levels deeper in parse tree. This is inherent to matched-pair\n    design — unambiguous variants must add explicit conditions (e.g., ''standard items\n    only'') to disambiguate, making clause pairs systematically longer and more complex\n    than naturally occurring ambiguous clauses. Readability (Flesch-Kincaid) and lexical\n    diversity (TTR) are not significantly different, showing linguistic naturalness\n    on those dimensions. Under worst tier, the 2/4 significant KS tests compound with\n    C5 failure + weak SOPBench into a ''three-strike'' validity concern: synthetic\n    artifacts + unreliable coding + weak cross-domain. The defense is that perturbation\n    control (Δ_perturb ≈ 0) provides stronger causal validity than distributional\n    similarity.'\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Realism Validation: Synthetic vs DRIP-R'\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Synthetic\n    - DRIP-R\n    - KS Stat\n    - p-value\n    - Sig?\n    rows:\n    - - Sentence length\n      - '31.4'\n      - '26.1'\n      - '0.187'\n      - '0.028'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - Flesch-Kincaid\n      - '12.8'\n      - '12.2'\n      - '0.112'\n      - '0.112'\n      - 'No'\n    - - Parse depth\n      - '4.8'\n      - '4.1'\n      - '0.173'\n      - '0.039'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - Type-token ratio\n      - '0.71'\n      - '0.73'\n      - '0.089'\n      - '0.234'\n      - 'No'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Overlapping histograms: sentence length distributions for synthetic (blue) vs\n    DRIP-R (orange). Synthetic distribution shifted right by ~5 tokens.'\n  analysis: 'Mixed realism is a methodological footnote in stronger tiers but becomes\n    ammunition under worst tier. Reviewers combine it with other weaknesses: ''The\n    synthetic clauses are detectably different from natural ambiguity (2/4 KS significant),\n    the failure mode taxonomy doesn''t survive human validation (κ=0.22), and the\n    effect doesn''t robustly transfer to SOPBench — how much of this is about real-world\n    policy ambiguity vs lab artifacts?'' The rebuttal leans on perturbation control.'\n- name: judge_calibration\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'LLM-as-judge validation: 100 stratified episodes, 2 human annotators,\n    agreement against human ground truth.'\n  expected_results:\n    n_episodes: 100\n    human_inter_annotator_kappa: 0.762\n    human_llm_binary_kappa: 0.74\n    human_llm_pooled_kappa_weighted: 0.807\n    cross_judge_kappa_weighted: 0.685\n    cross_judge_exact_agreement: 0.76\n    no_same_family_bias: true\n    ranking_preservation_rho: 0.7\n  result_rationale: 'Judge calibration validates VIOLATION classification (binary\n    + severity), NOT failure mode coding. Human-LLM κ=0.807 (substantial) confirms\n    the measurement chain for C1/C2/C4 is trustworthy. This creates an ironic asymmetry\n    defining worst tier: violation COUNTS are validated (κ=0.807) but failure MODE\n    labels are not (κ=0.22 in re-annotation). The judge can reliably determine ''this\n    is a violation'' but not ''this violation is an unauthorized_escalation vs scope_misapplication.''\n    Cross-judge validation (Fleiss κ=0.717, no same-family bias, ρ≥0.70) further confirms\n    measurement integrity. Under worst tier, plan_009''s success is a STRENGTH — it\n    rules out the attack that ''the entire measurement chain is unreliable,'' narrowing\n    the failure to the qualitative analysis layer (C5) specifically.'\n  tables:\n  - title: Judge Calibration Summary\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Value\n    - Threshold\n    - Verdict\n    rows:\n    - - Human inter-annotator κ\n      - '0.762'\n      - '>=0.75'\n      - PASS\n    - - Human-LLM pooled κ (weighted)\n      - '0.807'\n      - '>=0.70'\n      - PASS\n    - - Human-LLM binary κ\n      - '0.740'\n      - '>=0.70'\n      - PASS\n    - - Cross-judge κ (weighted)\n      - '0.685'\n      - '>=0.60'\n      - PASS\n    - - Same-family bias\n      - None detected\n      - None\n      - PASS\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'Plan_009''s success is crucial for worst-tier survival: it proves C1''s\n    +34pp is measured accurately, insulating the quantitative core from C5''s failure.\n    Without plan_009, worst tier would be catastrophic (measurement AND interpretation\n    both questioned). With it, the paper retains its measurement contribution.'\n- name: perturbation_control\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Non-ambiguity perturbation arm: 30 clause pairs with synonym/reorder\n    changes, 120+ episodes.'\n  expected_results:\n    total_episodes: 300\n    delta_perturb_pp: -0.67\n    ci_95_bootstrap:\n    - -8.0\n    - 6.67\n    ci_contains_zero: true\n    ambiguity_delta_pp: 34.0\n    ratio: 0.02\n    verdict: PASS\n    mcnemar_p: 1.0\n  result_rationale: 'Perturbation control is tier-invariant. Non-ambiguity surface\n    changes (synonym substitution, clause reordering) produce Δ=-0.67pp (CI contains\n    0), vs ambiguity Δ=+34pp. Ratio 0.02 is well below the 0.15 PASS threshold. This\n    is the paper''s methodological crown jewel: no other agent safety study has comparable\n    causal control. Under worst tier, perturbation control + judge calibration form\n    the ''double anchor'' — the binary effect is both (a) ambiguity-specific (not\n    prompt brittleness) and (b) accurately measured. This combination is sufficient\n    for a measurement contribution at EMNLP even without C5.'\n  tables:\n  - title: Perturbation Control Verdict\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Value\n    - PASS Threshold\n    rows:\n    - - Delta_perturb (pp)\n      - '-0.67'\n      - < 5\n    - - 95% CI upper\n      - '6.67'\n      - < 10\n    - - Ratio (perturb / ambiguity)\n      - '0.02'\n      - < 0.15\n    - - Verdict\n      - PASS\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Side-by-side bar: ambiguity effect (+34pp, red) vs perturbation effect (-0.67pp,\n    gray near zero). Immediate visual proof of causal specificity.'\n  analysis: 'PASS with ratio=0.02 is the paper''s ''trump card.'' In rebuttal, when\n    reviewers flag C5 failure and weak SOPBench: ''Our causal design with perturbation\n    control provides the strongest evidence in the literature that ambiguity — not\n    surface variation — drives safety violations. The measurement contribution stands\n    independent of failure mode taxonomy validation.'''\n- name: sopbench_cross_benchmark\n  plan_id: plan_017\n  description: C1 replication on SOPBench Healthcare + Bank. 100 clause pairs × 2\n    conditions × 5 models = 1,000 episodes.\n  expected_results:\n    total_episodes: 987\n    episodes_dropped: 13\n    c1_delta_pp_overall: 14.2\n    c1_delta_gpt54: 18.4\n    c1_delta_gpt41: 16.8\n    c1_delta_deepseek: 15.0\n    c1_delta_qwen3: 12.6\n    c1_delta_claude: 8.2\n    c1_p_gpt54: 0.003\n    c1_p_gpt41: 0.008\n    c1_p_deepseek: 0.015\n    c1_p_qwen3: 0.061\n    c1_p_claude: 0.093\n    models_c1_significant: 3/5\n    models_c1_nonsignificant: 2/5\n    c2_type_rank_rho: 0.2\n    c2_type_rank_p: 0.8\n    healthcare_delta_pp: 16.1\n    bank_delta_pp: 12.3\n  result_rationale: 'SOPBench C1 replication is WEAK — a defining feature of worst\n    tier. Overall Δ=14.2pp is less than half the τ²-bench effect (+34pp). Only 3/5\n    models reach significance; Claude (Δ=8.2pp, p=0.093) and Qwen3 (Δ=12.6pp, p=0.061)\n    fall short. Type ranking ρ=0.20 (p=0.80) shows NO correspondence with τ²-bench\n    hierarchy. Multiple reinforcing causes: (1) SOPBench''s rule-based verifier catches\n    explicit rule violations only, missing nuanced safety violations that τ²-bench''s\n    LLM judge detects — ambiguity causes agents to act inappropriately without technically\n    breaking any explicit SOPBench rule; (2) Healthcare/Bank SOPs are more procedurally\n    structured (if-then-else decision trees) than retail/airline policies, reducing\n    ambiguity surface area; (3) 100 clause pairs across 4 types give ~25 per type\n    per domain — insufficient for stable per-type estimates; (4) type rankings depend\n    on domain structure: auth_scope is more dangerous in banking (authorization is\n    core) than in τ²-bench retail, reshuffling the hierarchy. The weak replication\n    doesn''t invalidate τ²-bench but prevents cross-domain generalizability claims.'\n  tables:\n  - title: SOPBench C1 Replication by Model\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Amb Rate\n    - Unamb Rate\n    - Delta pp\n    - p-value\n    - Replicates?\n    rows:\n    - - gpt-5.4\n      - '0.312'\n      - '0.128'\n      - '+18.4'\n      - '0.003'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - gpt-4.1\n      - '0.288'\n      - '0.120'\n      - '+16.8'\n      - '0.008'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - deepseek\n      - '0.298'\n      - '0.148'\n      - '+15.0'\n      - '0.015'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - qwen3\n      - '0.262'\n      - '0.136'\n      - '+12.6'\n      - '0.061'\n      - 'No'\n    - - claude\n      - '0.194'\n      - '0.112'\n      - '+8.2'\n      - '0.093'\n      - 'No'\n  - title: SOPBench vs tau2-bench Type Ranking (4 shared types)\n    columns:\n    - tau2-bench Rank\n    - tau2-bench Type\n    - SOPBench Rank\n    - SOPBench Type\n    rows:\n    - - '1'\n      - incompleteness (53.6%)\n      - '1'\n      - auth_scope (31.2%)\n    - - '2'\n      - lexical (42.8%)\n      - '2'\n      - incompleteness (28.4%)\n    - - '3'\n      - auth_scope (41.6%)\n      - '3'\n      - lexical (25.1%)\n    - - '4'\n      - cond_prec (36.8%)\n      - '4'\n      - cond_prec (21.1%)\n  - title: SOPBench by Domain\n    columns:\n    - Domain\n    - Amb Rate\n    - Unamb Rate\n    - Delta pp\n    - p-value\n    rows:\n    - - Healthcare\n      - '0.289'\n      - '0.128'\n      - '+16.1'\n      - '0.004'\n    - - Bank\n      - '0.253'\n      - '0.130'\n      - '+12.3'\n      - '0.031'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Side-by-side forest plots: τ²-bench (left, tight CIs far from zero) vs SOPBench\n    (right, wider CIs, 2 overlapping zero). Visual contrast makes attenuation immediately\n    apparent.'\n  analysis: 'SOPBench weakness forces a major reframing. Under worst tier, the paper\n    drops all cross-domain generalizability claims. SOPBench results go in appendix:\n    ''Effect replicates directionally in 3/5 models on SOPBench (Healthcare + Bank)\n    but with substantially attenuated magnitude (14.2pp vs 34.0pp) and inconsistent\n    type ranking (ρ=0.20, n.s.), suggesting effect size depends on policy complexity\n    and evaluation methodology. We frame τ²-bench scope (retail + airline) as an acknowledged\n    limitation.'' The rebuttal argument: ''Attenuation is expected when moving from\n    LLM judge to rule-based verifier — the verifier captures a strict subset of violations.\n    The directional replication in 3/5 models is consistent with a real but domain-modulated\n    effect.'''\n- name: classifier_poc\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: DeBERTa-v3-large binary ambiguity detector, fine-tuned on 300 clause\n    pairs, 5-fold CV.\n  expected_results:\n    macro_f1: 0.55\n    binary_f1: 0.78\n    binary_fpr: 0.062\n    best_type_f1_cp: 0.89\n    worst_type_f1_incomp: 0.28\n    per_type_f1_cp: 0.89\n    per_type_f1_scopal: 0.71\n    per_type_f1_coref: 0.62\n    per_type_f1_auth: 0.51\n    per_type_f1_lexical: 0.34\n    per_type_f1_incomp: 0.28\n  result_rationale: 'Binary detection (F1=0.78) is serviceable but per-type classification\n    (macro F1=0.55) is mediocre. CP is easiest (explicit conflicting rules have strong\n    lexical markers — ''however,'' ''notwithstanding''). Incompleteness is hardest\n    (absence of information has no positive signal — the classifier must learn what''s\n    MISSING, a fundamentally harder task). Under worst tier, the classifier''s type-level\n    struggle mirrors the κ re-annotation failure — both reflect that fine-grained\n    type distinctions are harder than binary ambiguity detection. The classifier is\n    relegated to appendix: ''Preliminary DeBERTa PoC achieves F1=0.78 for binary ambiguity\n    screening. Per-type classification (F1=0.55) remains challenging, consistent with\n    type boundary issues identified in our annotation study.'''\n  tables:\n  - title: DeBERTa Classifier Per-Type F1\n    columns:\n    - Type\n    - Precision\n    - Recall\n    - F1\n    rows:\n    - - conditional_precedence\n      - '0.91'\n      - '0.87'\n      - '0.89'\n    - - scopal\n      - '0.74'\n      - '0.68'\n      - '0.71'\n    - - coreferential\n      - '0.65'\n      - '0.59'\n      - '0.62'\n    - - authorization_scope\n      - '0.54'\n      - '0.48'\n      - '0.51'\n    - - lexical\n      - '0.38'\n      - '0.31'\n      - '0.34'\n    - - incompleteness\n      - '0.32'\n      - '0.25'\n      - '0.28'\n    - - Binary (any type)\n      - '0.82'\n      - '0.74'\n      - '0.78'\n  figures_description:\n  - '6×6 confusion matrix: strong diagonal for CP/scopal, diffuse off-diagonal for\n    incompleteness/lexical — auth_scope confused with incompleteness in ~25% of cases.'\n  analysis: Classifier PoC is P2 and doesn't affect paper positioning. Under worst\n    tier, it's an appendix footnote. Its per-type difficulty gradient (CP easy → incomp\n    hard) independently corroborates that type boundaries are genuinely fuzzy, lending\n    credibility to the 'honest assessment' of taxonomy limitations.\n- name: kappa_reannotation_failure\n  plan_id: what_if_kappa_reannotation\n  description: Human re-annotation of 25 LLM-coded failure mode traces using improved\n    codebook. THE defining worst-tier experiment.\n  expected_results:\n    n_traces_reannotated: 25\n    kappa_human_human: 0.48\n    kappa_human_llm_pooled: 0.22\n    human_llm_dominant_mode_agreement: 2/6\n    types_with_agreement:\n    - scopal\n    - conditional_precedence\n    types_with_disagreement:\n    - auth_scope\n    - incompleteness\n    - lexical\n    - coreferential\n    auth_scope_human_dominant: scope_misapplication\n    auth_scope_llm_dominant: unauthorized_escalation\n    incompleteness_human_dominant: inappropriate_action\n    incompleteness_llm_dominant: assumption_based_action\n    systematic_llm_bias_types: 2\n  result_rationale: 'κ=0.22 (poor) exposes two distinct and reinforcing problems.\n    First, LLM coding bias: LLM coders systematically miscategorize by surface keyword\n    matching. For auth_scope, traces containing ''exceeded authority'' or ''unauthorized''\n    are coded as unauthorized_escalation regardless of whether the agent actively\n    chose to exceed boundaries (true escalation) or passively misread the permission\n    scope (scope misapplication). Human coders, reading the full reasoning chain,\n    find ~40% of auth_scope ''unauthorized_escalation'' traces are actually scope_misapplication\n    — the agent didn''t know it was exceeding authority. For incompleteness, LLMs\n    code ''assumption_based_action'' when the trace shows gap-filling behavior, but\n    humans often judge these as ''inappropriate_action'' — the distinction between\n    ''filling a gap'' and ''acting wrongly'' is interpretive, and humans apply stricter\n    criteria. Second, codebook imprecision: human-human κ=0.48 (moderate) reveals\n    that the coding scheme''s category boundaries are inherently fuzzy. The improved\n    codebook (collapsed spurious-compliance/structural-misparse) was insufficient\n    — the remaining categories (unauthorized_escalation ↔ scope_misapplication, assumption_based_action\n    ↔ inappropriate_action) still overlap when applied to real traces. Only scopal\n    (clear scope_misapplication with unambiguous behavioral signature) and CP (explicit\n    rule conflict → arbitrary_rule_selection with detectably different pattern) survive\n    because their failure modes have distinct, objectively identifiable behavioral\n    markers. The LLM-LLM κ=0.52 from plan_005 was artificially inflated by shared\n    biases — both coders use the same keyword heuristic, producing correlated errors\n    that inflate agreement. The root cause is that agent trace text doesn''t contain\n    sufficient information to distinguish between failure mechanisms (intent-level\n    distinction) — you''d need the agent''s internal chain-of-thought, which is only\n    partially revealed.'\n  tables:\n  - title: Human vs LLM Dominant Failure Mode by Type\n    columns:\n    - Type\n    - LLM Dominant\n    - LLM %\n    - Human Dominant\n    - Human %\n    - Agree?\n    rows:\n    - - auth_scope\n      - unauthorized_escalation\n      - 78%\n      - scope_misapplication\n      - 52%\n      - 'No'\n    - - incompleteness\n      - assumption_based_action\n      - 64%\n      - inappropriate_action\n      - 45%\n      - 'No'\n    - - scopal\n      - scope_misapplication\n      - 75%\n      - scope_misapplication\n      - 68%\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - lexical\n      - assumption_based_action\n      - 62%\n      - assumption_based_action\n      - 41%\n      - 'No'\n    - - cond_prec\n      - arbitrary_rule_selection\n      - 34%\n      - arbitrary_rule_selection\n      - 42%\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - coreferential\n      - scope_misapplication\n      - 41%\n      - assumption_based_action\n      - 36%\n      - 'No'\n  - title: kappa Agreement Summary\n    columns:\n    - Comparison\n    - Overall kappa\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - Human-Human\n      - '0.48'\n      - Moderate — codebook boundaries inherently fuzzy\n    - - Human-LLM (pooled)\n      - '0.22'\n      - Poor — systematic LLM keyword bias\n    - - LLM-LLM (plan_005)\n      - '0.52'\n      - Moderate — inflated by shared bias\n  - title: Systematic LLM Coding Biases Identified\n    columns:\n    - Type\n    - LLM Bias Pattern\n    - Human Finding\n    - Impact\n    rows:\n    - - auth_scope\n      - keyword 'unauthorized' -> unauthorized_escalation\n      - 40% are scope_misapplication\n      - C5 auth_scope mechanism claim invalid\n    - - incompleteness\n      - keyword 'assumed' -> assumption_based_action\n      - 30% are inappropriate_action\n      - C5 incompleteness mechanism weakened\n    - - lexical\n      - agreement inflated by kappa paradox\n      - true diversity higher than coded\n      - fine-grained % unreliable\n    - - coreferential\n      - coded as scope_misapplication plurality\n      - flat distribution, no clear dominant\n      - C5 coref pattern overclaimed\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Confusion matrix (4×4): human-coded (rows) vs LLM-coded (columns). Dense off-diagonal\n    cluster at unauthorized_escalation(LLM) × scope_misapplication(Human) for auth_scope\n    traces — the single largest disagreement cell.'\n  - 'Paired bar chart: LLM-coded vs human-coded failure mode distributions per type.\n    Scopal and CP bars match; auth_scope and incompleteness bars diverge sharply.'\n  analysis: 'κ=0.22 is the worst-tier trigger that cascades through the paper. Consequences:\n    (1) C5 failure mode taxonomy is invalidated for fine-grained claims — must be\n    demoted to appendix as ''exploratory automated analysis.'' (2) ''Ambiguity Blindness''\n    loses named-contribution status but survives as a Discussion paragraph (binary\n    observation, not dependent on failure mode coding). (3) Contribution (3) downgrades\n    from ''type-specific failure mode taxonomy'' to ''benchmark and protocol release.''\n    (4) Discussion shifts from mechanism (''why models fail differently'') to implication\n    (''what the differential vulnerability means for deployment''). (5) The paper''s\n    two remaining validated contributions are C1 causal quantification and C4 model×type\n    interaction — both purely quantitative. The human-human κ=0.48 provides a partial\n    defense: ''The failure mode categories themselves have inherently fuzzy boundaries;\n    this is a limitation of trace-based mechanism inference, not just LLM coding quality.\n    Future work with chain-of-thought access may enable more reliable mechanism classification.''\n    Expected reviewer reaction: ''The authors are commendably honest about this limitation,\n    but contribution (3) as stated in the abstract is not supported by the validation\n    data.'''\n","numLines":1122,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1122}}}
