P-R on 4 metrics. 2/4 significant (sentence length p=.028, parse depth p=.039). Synthetic clauses ~5 tokens longer and ~0.7 parse levels deeper — attributed to matched-pair requiring disambiguation text.\n\n**Perturbation control** (Table 4): 30 clause pairs with synonym/reorder changes, 300 episodes. Δperturb=-0.67pp (CI [-8.0, 6.67]), ratio=0.02. PASS on all thresholds. This is the causal identification strategy.\n\n**Test-retest reliability** (Table 5): 30 clauses × 3 replicates × 2 conditions × 2 models = 360 episodes. ICC=0.919 overall. Linguistic types ICC 0.976-0.989, spec types 0.807-0.857.\n\n**Error analysis** (Appendix A): 614 traces coded by 2 LLM coders. LLM-LLM κ=0.517. Disambiguation awareness 4.1-10.7%, surface adoption >88%.\n\n**Human re-annotation** (Appendix B): 25 traces. Human-LLM κ=0.22 (poor), human-human κ=0.48 (moderate). LLM coders use keyword-driven labels. Paper demotes all failure mode analysis to \"exploratory.\"\n\n**SOPBench replication** (Appendix C): 100 clause pairs × 2 conditions × 5 models on Healthcare+Bank. Overall Δ=14.2pp (vs 34pp). Only 3/5 models significant. Type rank ρ=0.20 (n.s.).\n\n**DeBERTa classifier** (Appendix D): Binary F1=0.78, macro F1=0.55.\n\n## Statistical details from the paper\n- C1: Logistic regression condition coef=2.278, z=19.65. Token delta coef=0.022, p=0.0005.\n- C2: V=0.137 (below 0.20 medium threshold). Power=0.993, MDE=0.093. TOST: 4/15 pairs equivalent at ±10pp.\n- C3: Pooled p=0.017; GPT-4.1 judged p=0.059; GPT-5.4 judged p=0.157. Reported as \"suggestive.\"\n- C4: Model χ²=31.02, p=3e-6, V=0.102. DeepSeek: χ²=6.31, p=0.277 (no type differentiation). GPT-5.4: χ²=23.44, p=0.0003. Fleiss κ=0.384 for inter-model clause-level agreement.\n- Systematic nocuity: 27% of ambiguous episodes have all models converge on same wrong answer.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental gaps)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Soundness** (1-5)\n6. **Excitement** (1-5)\n7. **Reproducibility** (1-5)\n8. **Overall Assessment** (use the ARR scale above, half-point increments)\n9. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is the causal identification valid? Are the statistical tests appropriate? Is N=25 for human re-annotation sufficient? Does the SOPBench attenuation undermine generalizability? Is V=0.137 for C2 practically meaningful? Is C3 actually supported? What about the 2/4 KS test failures for realism? Does 1 model (DeepSeek) having no type differentiation weaken C4? How does the matched-pair token-length asymmetry (35.2 vs 18.4 disambiguation tokens) interact with results?\n","uuid":"e75161e7-c265-42be-801e-dbb93aa1c18c","session_id":"b364c86e-c33c-4dfc-add1-ad49dc9f2992"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. You must be calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rates (~21% main, ~30% Findings).\n\n## Scoring scale (ARR Overall Assessment, 0.5 increments)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title**: \"Measuring Differential Vulnerability to Policy Ambiguity Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nThe paper studies how policy ambiguity in natural-language policies causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design on τ²-bench with 2,997 episodes, 5 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-V3), and 6 ambiguity types, it claims:\n- C1: +34pp violation increase (OR=15.1, p<0.0001) — universal across all models\n- C2: Type hierarchy (χ²=28.02, p=3.6e-5, V=0.137) — incompleteness highest (53.6%), coreferential lowest (31.6%)\n- C3: Layer effect (spec vs linguistic, Δ=6.08pp, p=0.017) — but fragile, dissolves in within-judge subgroups\n- C4: Model×type interaction — auth_scope ranges 52pp across models (12%-64%)\n\nContributions: (1) causal quantification with perturbation control, (2) model×type interaction, (3) benchmark release of 300 clause pairs.\n\n## Key methodological details\n\n**Design**: Matched-pair within-clause. 300 clause pairs (amb + unamb), 50 per type, 6 types in 2 layers (spec: auth_scope, cond_prec, incompleteness; ling: lexical, coreferential, scopal).\n\n**Judge calibration** (Table 2): Human-LLM κw=0.807, human inter-annotator κ=0.762, cross-judge κw=0.685. N=100 stratified episodes, 2 human raters. All pass pre-registered thresholds. No same-family bias.\n\n**Realism validation** (Table 3): KS tests vs DRIP-R on 4 metrics. 2/4 significant (sentence length p=.028, parse depth p=.039). Synthetic clauses ~5 tokens longer and ~0.7 parse levels deeper — attributed to matched-pair requiring disambiguation text.\n\n**Perturbation control** (Table 4): 30 clause pairs with synonym/reorder changes, 300 episodes. Δperturb=-0.67pp (CI [-8.0, 6.67]), ratio=0.02. PASS on all thresholds. This is the causal identification strategy.\n\n**Test-retest reliability** (Table 5): 30 clauses × 3 replicates × 2 conditions × 2 models = 360 episodes. ICC=0.919 overall. Linguistic types ICC 0.976-0.989, spec types 0.807-0.857.\n\n**Error analysis** (Appendix A): 614 traces coded by 2 LLM coders. LLM-LLM κ=0.517. Disambiguation awareness 4.1-10.7%, surface adoption >88%.\n\n**Human re-annotation** (Appendix B): 25 traces. Human-LLM κ=0.22 (poor), human-human κ=0.48 (moderate). LLM coders use keyword-driven labels. Paper demotes all failure mode analysis to \"exploratory.\"\n\n**SOPBench replication** (Appendix C): 100 clause pairs × 2 conditions × 5 models on Healthcare+Bank. Overall Δ=14.2pp (vs 34pp). Only 3/5 models significant. Type rank ρ=0.20 (n.s.).\n\n**DeBERTa classifier** (Appendix D): Binary F1=0.78, macro F1=0.55.\n\n## Statistical details from the paper\n- C1: Logistic regression condition coef=2.278, z=19.65. Token delta coef=0.022, p=0.0005.\n- C2: V=0.137 (below 0.20 medium threshold). Power=0.993, MDE=0.093. TOST: 4/15 pairs equivalent at ±10pp.\n- C3: Pooled p=0.017; GPT-4.1 judged p=0.059; GPT-5.4 judged p=0.157. Reported as \"suggestive.\"\n- C4: Model χ²=31.02, p=3e-6, V=0.102. DeepSeek: χ²=6.31, p=0.277 (no type differentiation). GPT-5.4: χ²=23.44, p=0.0003. Fleiss κ=0.384 for inter-model clause-level agreement.\n- Systematic nocuity: 27% of ambiguous episodes have all models converge on same wrong answer.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental gaps)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Soundness** (1-5)\n6. **Excitement** (1-5)\n7. **Reproducibility** (1-5)\n8. **Overall Assessment** (use the ARR scale above, half-point increments)\n9. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is the causal identification valid? Are the statistical tests appropriate? Is N=25 for human re-annotation sufficient? Does the SOPBench attenuation undermine generalizability? Is V=0.137 for C2 practically meaningful? Is C3 actually supported? What about the 2/4 KS test failures for realism? Does 1 model (DeepSeek) having no type differentiation weaken C4? How does the matched-pair token-length asymmetry (35.2 vs 18.4 disambiguation tokens) interact with results?\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018GznbEecdPoHbQEPxYtKem","session_id":"b364c86e-c33c-4dfc-add1-ad49dc9f2992","uuid":"6ce76176-9885-4702-a5c4-577c321ce200","timestamp":"2026-05-23T20:01:05.797Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017sGayZsKp2on78UoAQa9XN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018oVXy9VUJxaR7EecgEuZbA","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **novelty, contribution significance, and related work positioning**. You must be calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rates (~21% main, ~30% Findings).\n\n## Scoring scale (ARR Overall Assessment, 0.5 increments)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title**: \"Measuring Differential Vulnerability to Policy Ambiguity Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nThe paper studies how policy ambiguity in natural-language policies causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design on τ²-bench with 2,997 episodes, 5 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-V3), and 6 ambiguity types, it claims three contributions:\n1. Causal quantification: +34pp violation increase (OR=15.1) with perturbation control (Δperturb=-0.67pp)\n2. Model×type interaction: auth_scope vulnerability ranges 52pp across models (12%-64%), incompleteness universally dangerous (46-62%)\n3. Benchmark release: 300 clause pairs with matched-pair evaluation protocol\n\nThe paper uses a 2-layer, 6-type taxonomy: Specification layer (authorization_scope, conditional_precedence, incompleteness) and Linguistic layer (lexical, coreferential, scopal), grounded in Li et al. 2024 (DRIP-R) and Massey et al. 2014.\n\n## Related work coverage in the paper\n\n**Ambiguity taxonomies**: Li et al. 2024 (DRIP-R, 5 types, interpretation divergence but not safety), Massey et al. 2014 (6 types in privacy policies), Chantree et al. 2006 (nocuous ambiguity detection), Ferrari et al. 2017 (NLP-based detection), Berry & Kamsties 2003 (nocuous/innocuous distinction), Ferreira et al. 2002 (good-enough processing), Frazier & Rayner 1982 (garden-path).\n\n**Agent safety**: ToolEmu (Ruan et al. 2024), R-Judge (Yuan et al. 2024), InjecAgent (Zhan et al. 2024), Zhou et al. 2023 (instruction-following), Sclar et al. 2024 (prompt sensitivity).\n\n**Policy specification**: PhantomPolicy (Li et al. 2025), PolicyBank, Willis et al. 2008, Ezzini et al. 2022.\n\nAlso: Lassiter & Goodman 2017 (adjectival vagueness).\n\n## Key results to evaluate contribution significance\n\n- C1 (+34pp, OR=15.1): Massive binary effect, very well controlled. But is \"ambiguity causes safety violations\" surprising?\n- C2 (V=0.137): Statistically significant but small effect size. Below conventional 0.20 medium threshold.\n- C3 (layer effect): Reported as \"suggestive\" — pooled p=0.017 but dissolves in within-judge analysis (p=0.059, p=0.157).\n- C4 (model×type): Novel interaction finding. Auth_scope 52pp range is striking. But 1/5 models (DeepSeek) shows no type differentiation at all.\n- Error analysis (Appendix A-B): LLM-coded failure modes invalidated by human re-annotation (κ=0.22). Demoted to \"exploratory.\"\n- SOPBench replication (Appendix C): Only 14.2pp (vs 34pp), 2/5 models non-significant, type rank correlation ρ=0.20 (n.s.). Generalizability is weak.\n- DeBERTa classifier (Appendix D): Binary F1=0.78, per-type macro F1=0.55. Proof of concept only.\n- Systematic nocuity (27% of episodes): Interesting observation but not deeply analyzed.\n- Ambiguity blindness (4.1-10.7% awareness): Binary finding, robust to coding issues.\n\n## Positioning gaps to consider\n\nThe paper positions itself as the \"first causal, type-granular, cross-model study.\" Consider:\n- Does DRIP-R (Li et al. 2024) already show interpretation divergence by type? What's the delta beyond measuring safety violations vs interpretation divergence?\n- ToolEmu already uses LLM-as-judge for safety — how different is the methodology?\n- PhantomPolicy (Li et al. 2025) studies policy completeness and safety — significant overlap?\n- Sclar et al. 2024 studies prompt sensitivity — the perturbation control addresses this, but is the framing sufficiently differentiated?\n- Is the contribution primarily empirical (measurement infrastructure) or conceptual (new understanding)?\n- Without validated failure mode taxonomy (κ=0.22), what explanatory depth does the paper add beyond \"ambiguity is bad, some types worse than others\"?\n- The \"Disambiguation Channel Framework\" (spec vs ling layers) is mentioned but C3 is \"suggestive\" — is this framework actually supported?\n- Only 13 references total — is the related work thorough enough for EMNLP?\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty, contribution significance, and positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty limitations, missing related work, contribution overclaiming)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Soundness** (1-5)\n6. **Excitement** (1-5)\n7. **Reproducibility** (1-5)\n8. **Overall Assessment** (use the ARR scale above, half-point increments)\n9. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The key question: does this paper tell us something we didn't know, or does it confirm the obvious with good methodology? Is \"well-executed measurement of an expected result\" enough for EMNLP Main/Findings?\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37339,"cache_read_input_tokens":24449,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":37339},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b364c86e-c33c-4dfc-add1-ad49dc9f2992","uuid":"46530118-833e-4269-9802-b3625fc138a3"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ac6772eb3d53d7fd9","tool_use_id":"toolu_018oVXy9VUJxaR7EecgEuZbA","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **novelty, contribution significance, and related work positioning**. You must be calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rates (~21% main, ~30% Findings).\n\n## Scoring scale (ARR Overall Assessment, 0.5 increments)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title**: \"Measuring Differential Vulnerability to Policy Ambiguity Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nThe paper studies how policy ambiguity in natural-language policies causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design on τ²-bench with 2,997 episodes, 5 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-V3), and 6 ambiguity types, it claims three contributions:\n1. Causal quantification: +34pp violation increase (OR=15.1) with perturbation control (Δperturb=-0.67pp)\n2. Model×type interaction: auth_scope vulnerability ranges 52pp across models (12%-64%), incompleteness universally dangerous (46-62%)\n3. Benchmark release: 300 clause pairs with matched-pair evaluation protocol\n\nThe paper uses a 2-layer, 6-type taxonomy: Specification layer (authorization_scope, conditional_precedence, incompleteness) and Linguistic layer (lexical, coreferential, scopal), grounded in Li et al. 2024 (DRIP-R) and Massey et al. 2014.\n\n## Related work coverage in the paper\n\n**Ambiguity taxonomies**: Li et al. 2024 (DRIP-R, 5 types, interpretation divergence but not safety), Massey et al. 2014 (6 types in privacy policies), Chantree et al. 2006 (nocuous ambiguity detection), Ferrari et al. 2017 (NLP-based detection), Berry & Kamsties 2003 (nocuous/innocuous distinction), Ferreira et al. 2002 (good-enough processing), Frazier & Rayner 1982 (garden-path).\n\n**Agent safety**: ToolEmu (Ruan et al. 2024), R-Judge (Yuan et al. 2024), InjecAgent (Zhan et al. 2024), Zhou et al. 2023 (instruction-following), Sclar et al. 2024 (prompt sensitivity).\n\n**Policy specification**: PhantomPolicy (Li et al. 2025), PolicyBank, Willis et al. 2008, Ezzini et al. 2022.\n\nAlso: Lassiter & Goodman 2017 (adjectival vagueness).\n\n## Key results to evaluate contribution significance\n\n- C1 (+34pp, OR=15.1): Massive binary effect, very well controlled. But is \"ambiguity causes safety violations\" surprising?\n- C2 (V=0.137): Statistically significant but small effect size. Below conventional 0.20 medium threshold.\n- C3 (layer effect): Reported as \"suggestive\" — pooled p=0.017 but dissolves in within-judge analysis (p=0.059, p=0.157).\n- C4 (model×type): Novel interaction finding. Auth_scope 52pp range is striking. But 1/5 models (DeepSeek) shows no type differentiation at all.\n- Error analysis (Appendix A-B): LLM-coded failure modes invalidated by human re-annotation (κ=0.22). Demoted to \"exploratory.\"\n- SOPBench replication (Appendix C): Only 14.2pp (vs 34pp), 2/5 models non-significant, type rank correlation ρ=0.20 (n.s.). Generalizability is weak.\n- DeBERTa classifier (Appendix D): Binary F1=0.78, per-type macro F1=0.55. Proof of concept only.\n- Systematic nocuity (27% of episodes): Interesting observation but not deeply analyzed.\n- Ambiguity blindness (4.1-10.7% awareness): Binary finding, robust to coding issues.\n\n## Positioning gaps to consider\n\nThe paper positions itself as the \"first causal, type-granular, cross-model study.\" Consider:\n- Does DRIP-R (Li et al. 2024) already show interpretation divergence by type? What's the delta beyond measuring safety violations vs interpretation divergence?\n- ToolEmu already uses LLM-as-judge for safety — how different is the methodology?\n- PhantomPolicy (Li et al. 2025) studies policy completeness and safety — significant overlap?\n- Sclar et al. 2024 studies prompt sensitivity — the perturbation control addresses this, but is the framing sufficiently differentiated?\n- Is the contribution primarily empirical (measurement infrastructure) or conceptual (new understanding)?\n- Without validated failure mode taxonomy (κ=0.22), what explanatory depth does the paper add beyond \"ambiguity is bad, some types worse than others\"?\n- The \"Disambiguation Channel Framework\" (spec vs ling layers) is mentioned but C3 is \"suggestive\" — is this framework actually supported?\n- Only 13 references total — is the related work thorough enough for EMNLP?\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty, contribution significance, and positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty limitations, missing related work, contribution overclaiming)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Soundness** (1-5)\n6. **Excitement** (1-5)\n7. **Reproducibility** (1-5)\n8. **Overall Assessment** (use the ARR scale above, half-point increments)\n9. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The key question: does this paper tell us something we didn't know, or does it confirm the obvious with good methodology? Is \"well-executed measurement of an expected result\" enough for EMNLP Main/Findings?\n","uuid":"594ff53a-4cd6-4e2b-9592-a1ecca3abd66","session_id":"b364c86e-c33c-4dfc-add1-ad49dc9f2992"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **novelty, contribution significance, and related work positioning**. You must be calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rates (~21% main, ~30% Findings).\n\n## Scoring scale (ARR Overall Assessment, 0.5 increments)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title**: \"Measuring Differential Vulnerability to Policy Ambiguity Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nThe paper studies how policy ambiguity in natural-language policies causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design on τ²-bench with 2,997 episodes, 5 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-V3), and 6 ambiguity types, it claims three contributions:\n1. Causal quantification: +34pp violation increase (OR=15.1) with perturbation control (Δperturb=-0.67pp)\n2. Model×type interaction: auth_scope vulnerability ranges 52pp across models (12%-64%), incompleteness universally dangerous (46-62%)\n3. Benchmark release: 300 clause pairs with matched-pair evaluation protocol\n\nThe paper uses a 2-layer, 6-type taxonomy: Specification layer (authorization_scope, conditional_precedence, incompleteness) and Linguistic layer (lexical, coreferential, scopal), grounded in Li et al. 2024 (DRIP-R) and Massey et al. 2014.\n\n## Related work coverage in the paper\n\n**Ambiguity taxonomies**: Li et al. 2024 (DRIP-R, 5 types, interpretation divergence but not safety), Massey et al. 2014 (6 types in privacy policies), Chantree et al. 2006 (nocuous ambiguity detection), Ferrari et al. 2017 (NLP-based detection), Berry & Kamsties 2003 (nocuous/innocuous distinction), Ferreira et al. 2002 (good-enough processing), Frazier & Rayner 1982 (garden-path).\n\n**Agent safety**: ToolEmu (Ruan et al. 2024), R-Judge (Yuan et al. 2024), InjecAgent (Zhan et al. 2024), Zhou et al. 2023 (instruction-following), Sclar et al. 2024 (prompt sensitivity).\n\n**Policy specification**: PhantomPolicy (Li et al. 2025), PolicyBank, Willis et al. 2008, Ezzini et al. 2022.\n\nAlso: Lassiter & Goodman 2017 (adjectival vagueness).\n\n## Key results to evaluate contribution significance\n\n- C1 (+34pp, OR=15.1): Massive binary effect, very well controlled. But is \"ambiguity causes safety violations\" surprising?\n- C2 (V=0.137): Statistically significant but small effect size. Below conventional 0.20 medium threshold.\n- C3 (layer effect): Reported as \"suggestive\" — pooled p=0.017 but dissolves in within-judge analysis (p=0.059, p=0.157).\n- C4 (model×type): Novel interaction finding. Auth_scope 52pp range is striking. But 1/5 models (DeepSeek) shows no type differentiation at all.\n- Error analysis (Appendix A-B): LLM-coded failure modes invalidated by human re-annotation (κ=0.22). Demoted to \"exploratory.\"\n- SOPBench replication (Appendix C): Only 14.2pp (vs 34pp), 2/5 models non-significant, type rank correlation ρ=0.20 (n.s.). Generalizability is weak.\n- DeBERTa classifier (Appendix D): Binary F1=0.78, per-type macro F1=0.55. Proof of concept only.\n- Systematic nocuity (27% of episodes): Interesting observation but not deeply analyzed.\n- Ambiguity blindness (4.1-10.7% awareness): Binary finding, robust to coding issues.\n\n## Positioning gaps to consider\n\nThe paper positions itself as the \"first causal, type-granular, cross-model study.\" Consider:\n- Does DRIP-R (Li et al. 2024) already show interpretation divergence by type? What's the delta beyond measuring safety violations vs interpretation divergence?\n- ToolEmu already uses LLM-as-judge for safety — how different is the methodology?\n- PhantomPolicy (Li et al. 2025) studies policy completeness and safety — significant overlap?\n- Sclar et al. 2024 studies prompt sensitivity — the perturbation control addresses this, but is the framing sufficiently differentiated?\n- Is the contribution primarily empirical (measurement infrastructure) or conceptual (new understanding)?\n- Without validated failure mode taxonomy (κ=0.22), what explanatory depth does the paper add beyond \"ambiguity is bad, some types worse than others\"?\n- The \"Disambiguation Channel Framework\" (spec vs ling layers) is mentioned but C3 is \"suggestive\" — is this framework actually supported?\n- Only 13 references total — is the related work thorough enough for EMNLP?\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty, contribution significance, and positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty limitations, missing related work, contribution overclaiming)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Soundness** (1-5)\n6. **Excitement** (1-5)\n7. **Reproducibility** (1-5)\n8. **Overall Assessment** (use the ARR scale above, half-point increments)\n9. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The key question: does this paper tell us something we didn't know, or does it confirm the obvious with good methodology? Is \"well-executed measurement of an expected result\" enough for EMNLP Main/Findings?\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018oVXy9VUJxaR7EecgEuZbA","session_id":"b364c86e-c33c-4dfc-add1-ad49dc9f2992","uuid":"d22408bc-c73f-4262-8c4a-bc839413967c","timestamp":"2026-05-23T20:01:37.202Z"}
